From: Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com>
Subject: Re: It's official.  DEC is no more.
Date: 1998/06/28
Message-ID: <359700E8.85B2E2E6@idirect.com>
X-Deja-AN: 366970921
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <35802C29.58EC@pfizer.com> <35857085.7EBB@world.std.com> <6m5c9i$9aq@flonk.uk.sun.com> <3586F049.5BE5@EasyInternet.net> <6m888h$bfq@crchh14.us.nortel.com>
MIME-Version: 1.0
To: Steve Simmons <simmons@nortel.ca>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: "Usenet User"
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


>Steve Simmons wrote:

> >   In reality the seeds for DEC's problems was sown with the enormous
> >   success of the VAX.  Mr Olsen and the rest of the "VAX mafia" was
> >   too slow to see the technological discontinuity offered by RISC
> >   and then indecisively floundered around for too long afterwards.
> BINGO!!!!   In the late 80's, many of the smarter powers at DEC (me excluded)
> understood that RISC had a better long term viability than the VAX. In fact,
> Dave Cutler was driving that effort; however, nobody wanted to kill the goose that laid
> the golden eggs.  Each VAX sale had a high profit margin, and DEC built
> a cost structure to mirror this.

Jerome Fine replies:

Perhaps the entire DEC management were totally blind-sided by the
profit margins that they were able to sustain with both the PDP-11 and
VAX lines.  I have told this joke(?) before and I suspect that the
figures are not too far from the truth.

Q: What was DEC's marketing policy during the mid 1980s?
A:  Add nothing!
      As an example, The RD53 was a disk drive from Micropolis
      (a 1325 if I remember correctly).  Perhaps DEC purchased
      many thousands for about $ 500 and if I remember correctly
      re-sold them with the DEC RD53 label for about $ 5000.
      Now if you just notice that the only difference between these
      two prices is the zero on the end, you can easily see that
      DEC did indeed add "nothing" (well they did add another
      label) when the drive was re-sold as an RD53.
Q: What was DEC's hardware policy during the mid 1980s?
A:  Add nothing!
     As an example, to convert the Micropolis from a 1325 to
     an RD53, a jumper was required to allow the RQDX1,2,3
     to recognize the drive as a DEC RD53 drive.  It was easier
     to machine stuff a zero-ohm resistor (nothing again) onto
     the logic board rather than set up a jumper.  So again, DEC
     added nothing, even on the hardware side.

Now, both Q and A are stated tongue in cheek, especially now
that DEC is no longer.  From my point of view, DEC produced
a CPU design second to none.  And most of their operating
design concepts and architecture were the best I have seen.
The problem seems to be that they were stuck in the low
volume (by today's standards) high margin (by anyone's
standards -1000% markup was considered normal?)
business.  The other side of the problem was that it took
far too long to produce enhancements and fix bugs in the
operating system software in most cases.  While this is
a disease in the industry (especially for Microsoft), I do
think that DEC had at least an equal share, if not more.
Of course, their operating systems became stable over
time due to many releases and eventually (especially in
the case of VMS) became very rich and almost bug-free.
This strategy of releasing many updates was excellent
from the stability point of view of the software plus
had the added advantage of generating large profits
from the ongoing support and update fees.  I am not
sure who benefited more in the long run - DEC from
the stability of revenue or the users from the stability of
an excellent product.  Certainly, we will have to wait to
see if Microsoft does the same with Windows 98.
But DEC still severely limited the size of the user
community by demanding far too high a price to fix
bugs in the operating systems.  Of course, now
that Microsoft has a virtual monopoly on the operating
system software, we note that the price of the update
to Windows 95 is starting to increase.  It is still nowhere
near the prices that DEC charged for PDP-11 operating
systems, let alone what DEC charges for VMS.  But
Microsoft is selling volumes that are 1,000 times (10,000? -
anyone have the figures) the number of copies of VMS
that were ever sold.

> RISC was a low profit margin business, and DEC needed to restructure
> the entire company.   Our president at Convex was the first to boast about
> this as the likely problem.  Well, Convex is now HP but his point is still
> well taken.  This is especially true since Convex's management was transplanted
> from Mostek (with the exception of Bob Palmer who went somewhere else) ;-).

I would suggest that both AT&T and IBM likely had similar problems.  In
the case of AT&T, they were fortunate (from the long term point of view
and from the customer side) to be broken up.  If IBM would have been
able to go through that same process, likely many of the re-structured
components could be as large as IBM is right now by itself.  DEC, in
my opinion could have "enjoyed" the same process as AT&T.  Each
of the PDP-11, VAX and Alpha groups would have been best off
as separate companies, each competing with each other on a truly
independent basis.  Perhaps even VMS could have been separate as
well since by now (hearsay?) it would already have been able to run
on a PC and have become as much of a commodity as Windows 98.
I don't really think that Ken Olsen was wrong when (if I remember
correctly - I think I once saw a review of a speech made by
Ken Olsen in around 1985 - in DEC professional?) he stated that
DEC should have become a software only company.  I suspect
that might have been modified to be an Operating System/CPU
only company, but DEC is now gone and we will never know.

> I work for NORTEL now, and I am seeing the same major technological transition
> from circuit based to packet switched technologies.  Oh well, the only thing
> that is constant is change!!!!

Hope you do well at NORTEL.  All we can do now is watch the
Intel/Microsoft combo.  It would have been interesting if there
had been an independent VMS not tied to any hardware.  As
a force on its own, if VMS could have gained sufficient market
share when it had the chance, I have little doubt that Intel
would have made any necessary adjustments to the hardware
to accommodate a VMS on the PC.  If not, AMD would likely
have been able to do so.  As it is, we will now have to rely
on Compaq to determine the fate of VMS.  Since I never
became heavily involved in VMS, I am watching from the
side lines.  My interest at the moment (and likely to be for
a long time) is RT-11 and presently I am focused on
the Y2K issues for Legacy Applications from 10 years
(and in some cases only 5 years) ago.  In some cases, the
programs no longer have up-to-date source files.  Lots
of fun.  Certain applications have also used up all the
available memory space and would need a very major
restructure effort to change to current versions of
the RT-11 operating system.  In these cases, it is
much easier to patch the few CUSPs (Commonly
Used System Programs like DIR and PIP) and add
the trivial extra instructions to the resident operating
system (if even needed - even the distribution version
of the operating system does not rollover the date
at the end of the month).  For one system I helped,
the clock board (Codar) was generating the correct
values, but the person who wrote the software less
than 10 years ago did not bother to go beyond 1999.
Trivial to fix when first written.  Easy, but time consuming
now.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict




