In article <hshubs-2706981803220001@user-38lcg3j.dialup.mindspring.com>, hshubs@mindspring.com (Howard S Shubs) writes:
>In article <1998Jun27.133948.1@eisner>, Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam wrote:
>
>>Certainly writing things in comp.os.vms does not get the message to
>>Compaq top management.  The list of vice presidents chosen for the
>>new company did not include Stephen Hoffman :-)
>
>Well, who -should- we write?  We need names and addresses.
>
>-- 

I posted for that info a while back, and nobody came back with the answer.
Somwhere between EP and our favorite online engineers their must be some
chain of command, but damned if I could find out who it was composed of.
The only thing I know for sure is that Claflin isn't in it anymore.

As for getting their attention, if playing nice doesn't do it, there is 
always playing hardball.

The big players, who seem to be all that Compaq cares about (oddly, 
given that they sell mostly small machines) should immediately and publicly
announce a purchasing freeze of all large Alpha systems.  A little bit of a
press briefing where, say, MCI and a few others basically tell EP that if
workstations go, Galaxies is dead, should do wonders for Compaq stock
prices, and maybe put the fear of God back into EP.  It isn't even all that
illogical a thing for them to do.  Compaq is sending a message to these big
boys that it doesn't give a hoot if they won't be able to find trained
developers or systems operators for those big ticket systems a few years
down the road.  Or that it cares that they will need to spend 10X more than 
they should have for development systems. Or that they are pleased to
destroy any hope of much new software for these systems.  Hell, would you
sink millions into Galaxies level systems when the vendor is busily ripping
out the support structure with a machete? 

IBM will be very happy to have their business, and you know, might very
well be fielding Alpha systems of its own, since it looks like they might
be making the chips anyway.

You've got to love the future - all the vendors shove WNT in your face and
your only other option becomes Linux, but you can hardly get any software
for that other than small stuff because the major software houses are too
afraid of Microsoft to make Linux products.  So you get to choose between
shoveware and moldering security hole.  

Ok, so Compaq doesn't want to sell small OpenVMS machines, fine, just 
sell a license for the OS to somebody with the stipulation that "humongous
SMP machines are off limits."  How about it, Process, Mentec, somebody?

It's going to be really amusing when all the chip production lines start
failing when their old VAXes die and there is nothing small around to
replace them with.  Would you bet a million dollars of half made chips on 
WNT?  Doesn't Intel have a zillion VMS machines running its chip production
facilities?  Ooooooh, this is evil.  What would happen if Intel made a big
deal and publicly offered to buy OpenVMS from Compaq?!? Compaq is shoving
Alpha at Microsoft to try to break the Intel monopoly, so symmetry demands
that Intel stuff some OS back into Microsoft's face. And Intel needs small
OpenVMS machines anyway.  They've been leaning towards Novell, but OpenVMS
would be so poetic! If they offer enough the stockholders will force EP to
cough it up.  Merced is way the hell behind schedule, Intel will be making
Alphas anyway.  Imagine if you will, the look on Bill Gates' face when he
hears that Intel just offered 3-4 Billion cold cash for OpenVMS, and just
for kicks, also purchased Adobe and Corel. Does Compaq sell?  Who knows,
but the one thing that Microsoft simply could not do would be to counter
offer, as the FTC would never allow them to purchase the OS under those
conditions. 

Anybody from Intel reading this?  Care to get out your check books?

Regards.

David Mathog
