In article <1998Jun27.143841.1@sable>, pmoreau@cenaath.cena.dgac.fr (Patrick MOREAU, CENA Athis, Tel: 01.69.57.64.40) writes:
>Into the Compaq/Digital Merger Q&A available at URL 
>http://www.decus.org/decus/lugs/esilug , you can see the announcement of the
>END of OpenVMS support on Compaq/Digital workstations !! 
>
>Here is the original text:
>
>   Q: What are you plans to support customers still using VMS on Compaq
>   workstations?
>
>   A: We will continue to support OVMS on the currently shipping DIGITAL
>   Personal Workstation au-Series systems. As planned by DIGITAL before
>   the merger, support for OVMS on the workstation family will continue
>   until the year 2000. After that time, customers will be given a range
>   of options that includes migration to NT, migration to an AlphaServer
>   running OVMS or use of a custom solution that will be available to
>   them on a contractual basis for an indefinite period of time.

Brilliant!  Compaq spends billions to buy the remains of Digital, a company
which has just mismanaged itself out of existence, and along the way
alienated the vast majority of its former customers, and then, rather than
breaking with Digital's losing (and downright annoying) ways, continues on
with that grand (sic) tradition.  Perhaps somebody at Compaq should ask
themselves just exactly how many of the applications which run on Compaq's
Intel machines were developed on servers.  I bet if you look within Digital
itself you will find these very same, soon to be discontinued, workstations
sitting on the desks of every single OpenVMS software engineer.  Ditto for
every other software developer for the OS.  In fact, I'd bet there isn't a
single piece of OpenVMS software which isn't mostly developed on
workstations, even if the final target is a server.  Come on Compaq - if
you want to milk the cash cow you've got to give it something to eat! 

Also, anybody intending to "use a custom solution that will be available to
them on a contractual basis for an indefinite period of time" had best have
very deep pockets indeed.  Probably cheaper to hire your own ex-Digital
engineers and do it yourself. 

