One thing to keep in mind.

Compaq decided to buy DEC.
At first, they were going to keep it as a separate/independant subsidiary.
But then, something changed, and they decided to integrate DEC into Compaq.

Now, Compaq staff are probably going through a HUGE learning curve and
ex-DECies 
will have to transfer as much knowledge as possible to Compaq management so
that the
later can make intelligent decisions.

Remember that conventional knowledge says : if it runs on a "mainframe", it
doesn't run on a desktop. So it is perhaps understandable that Compaq may have
assumed that VMS wasn't really fit to run on a workstation, and this is easy
to sustain considering Palmer's unwillingness to
enable VMS to boot on cheap workstations.

I think that as Compaq finds out exactly what it has purchased, it will have
to re-adjust its 
philosophies. We just have to make sure Compaq knows what the true DEC
customers want instead
of what Bob GQ Palmer told Compaq.



