In article <qxp67hvg88k.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>, Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> writes:
|> 
|> I was a summer intern at DEC Eastern Research Lab (at hudson, long ago).
|> People from DEC-WRL, which produced Titan and MultiTitan, came and
|> gave a talk on Titan.
|> 
|> I think it ran Unix, because someone from the audience asked
|> "Does it run VNS". The answer ("No") seemed to make it dead
|> in many peoples eyes at that meeting.
|> 

We called the operating system "TUNIX".  It was perhaps the
only UNIX-like operating system partially written in Modula-2.
However, large parts were C code borrowed (almost) verbatim
from 4.something BSD, including most of the user-level code.

I spent several years maintaining TUNIX, including a major revision
of the networking code that convinced me that it is a mistake
to code only half of an operating system kernel in a type-safe
language.  (I spent weeks chasing down one very reclusive bug
due to a type mismatch between a Modula-2 caller and a C callee ...
or was it the other way around?)

At the time, very few other people had run UNIX systems with
128 MB of RAM, and we discovered some interesting things about
the BSD file-system buffer management code.  And it was pretty
cool to have a "personal computer" that ran ten times faster
than any workstation on the market at the time.

-Jeff



