gheston@ro.com (Gary Heston) wrote:

>In article <358C3A12.916B6D4@videotron.ca>,
>JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
>Yeah; too bad management can't comprehend the difference between serious
>enterprise software and overhyped enterprise junk.

I think the blame is more than just on "management". Corporate
structures can sometimes promote techno-idiots to management
positions. They are a major danger.

But techies refuse to breach the accounting bridge in most companies.
Management pays attention to numbers more than anything. Show how one
unit compares to five, and very often the numbers look more and more
reasonable. One of the big problems is not being able to communicate
issues to management. Have you ever watched a hard core tech try to
help a user. It is hilarious. Only IS departments care about
technicals the rest of the company cares about reliability, TOTAL COST
OF OWNERSHIP, future values. When technical departments start learning
something about real world needs those problems are avoided more. I
know I'm a techie. I also took business and managerial accounting,
I've managed to cross that bridge and have no problem make "sense" to
management instead of technical "nonsense". I've seen other technical
people fight battes in management and saw the eyes glaze over. 

You can sell management lots of stuff. I've seen people lie and sell
bleeding edge, untested, less reliable technology as a way to expand
our network and build a more reliable infrastructure. Management
bought it for $100,000. In the end they spent over $200,000 but
somebody didn't bother looking at TCO or all the related expenses and
missed the boat.

We are planning a migration from a VMS-based enterprise mail system
(which we are fairly certain shall become a worse off orphan now that
it is owned by a Compaq). The reliability of VMS and our emaail system
has spoiled our users. We can't go to Exchange/NT, it would be
ludicrous. A few examples of lost mail and downtime, reloading of the
operating system and Exchange to get things up again, are all the
ammunition we need. Couple those examples with a priced out scenario
of highly redundant server clusters to approach the reliability we
had, question the need for increase bandwidth between sites for
replication of whole information stores and we have a price scenario
that will scare management straight.





