In article <1998Jun27.143841.1@sable>, 
  pmoreau@cenaath.cena.dgac.fr writes:

> Into the Compaq/Digital Merger Q&A available at URL 
> http://www.decus.org/decus/lugs/esilug , you can see the announcement of the
> END of OpenVMS support on Compaq/Digital workstations !! 
> 
> 3) Migrate from an  OpeVMS workstation to an OpenVMS server is another
>    indication we (VMS customers) are cash cow : software is more expensive
>    on servers.
> 

   Not surprising, in the same Q&A we can find:

---

  Q: What are you plans to support customers still using VMS on Compaq servers?

  A: Compaq is committed to maintaining its lead in business-critical solutions
  by continuing the investment in OpenVMS and Tandem NonStop Himalaya.

---

  A couple of very interesting phrases here:

  "still using VMS" - the implication being that nobody will
want to start using VMS, Compaq is maintaining this legacy technology
for those of us who haven't been smart enough to switch to NT/Unix yet.

   "business-critical solutions" - this is usually marketing speak for
"what does it matter what it costs when your business lives or dies by it".
The gratuitous reference to Tandem ( which has no relevance to a question
about VMS ), suggests their mindset is to market VMS in concert with only
the highest-end systems.

   At the very least I would have wanted Compaq to respond to a question
like this with "we will continue to offer OpenVMS on every Alpha-based
server we sell". I think it's more likely that within a few years you
won't be able to buy any new VMS system for less than $100,000.

> 
> We MUST tell to Compaq we want be able to run OVMS on workstation as long as
> OVMS will be available from Compaq.
> 

   I doubt anyone there would care. While they're obviously interested in
appeasing a few multinationals with megabucks to spend on VMS I imagine their
view of the rest of us is that we're small potatoes, and that when they
drive us away from VMS we'll just be buying NT or Unix systems from them
anyway. I suspect the only satisfaction we can get at this point is to
do everything in our power to see to it that when we're forced to replace our
VMS systems with NT or Unix we don't select Compaq as the vendor.

   The day I can't get an affordable VMS system from Compaq ( ie in the
same price range as comparable NT and Unix systems ) will be the
last day I buy any Compaq product!

   I understand Sun owes their start to some degree to DEC-10/20 customers
who were shafted by Digital in the early 80s, who know which company might
benefit from disgruntled VMS customers.

> 
> If VMS stay inside Compaq, its name will soon be DeadVMS.
> 

   No, I think its name will be OpenVM$$$

