>
>I'd have thought you'd have been much better going for a system with
>native 8" drives and transferring the data over RS232.

Especially because the PDP-11's double-density 8" format is different from
the whole rest of the world and most non-DEC hardware can't do it (since it
uses SD headers with DD data fields and some minor bit twiddling to avoid
a potential gotcha).  However someone mentioned Punctum Transfer earlier,
they definitely know the difference so I'm sure if anyone can help, they
can.  And they're in Germany.

The PDP-11's RX01 format is vanilla though, so it can be read with your
favorite CP/M box, or with a PC that has an 8" drive attached.  The Micro
Solutions CompatiCard IV floppy controller can handle 8" drives, and California
Digital <http://cadigital.com> sells NOS 8" drives.  I have nothing to do with
any of the above-mentioned companies, just a satisfied Micro Sol'ns customer
and I've heard of the other two.

>Point is that the file
>format on the 8" won't be DOS type, let alone Win95 or WinNT, so you not
>only need software to control the drive, but some more software to get
>at the file-structured data.  (Driver and application, if you see what I
>mean?)

If the disks are in RT-11 format (which is very likely), my PUTR.COM program
can read SD disks directly on a PC, and can extract the files from DD disk
snapshots taken elsewhere.  <http://www.dbit.com/pub/putr/>.  This is for DOS,
it ought to work under W95 too, most likely not with WNT.

John Wilson
D Bit



