On Mon, 22 Jun 1998 16:54:35, Paul Sture <paul.sture@ubs.com> wrote:

> >    I  have  a  vague
> >     recollection  that  the dollar-sign was used in similar contexts  in
> >     other earlier DEC operating systems  (anyone  care  to  chime  in?).
> 
> I'm straining my memory cells here... I have a vague feeling the $ was used in
> RT11 and RSX, at the very least for system routines, although I don't think so
> for filenames.
> 
> As I remember, all you had to do was ensure that your subroutine names didn't
> have a $, and you were guaranteed that they would conflict with the names of
> system or compiler routines. This of course is still true on VMS:

Absolutely correct. The $ character is one of the non alaphabetic 
characters which is part of the Radix-50 character set. Rad-50 is a 
trick used to pack three characters into one 16-bit word. RSX-11m 
kernel and system routines used '$' and '.'. in their names to 
guarantee a non-conflicting namespace.

Cheers - Dave.



