Organization: neuron, technology consultants
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


On an Alpha system, to run Windows NT, you must have a minimum of two
partitions available if you plan to make a partition into NTFS.

The standard, as it is today, is to make SCSI Hard Drive 0, SCSI ID 0, the
disk that you will load the Operating System on.

Newer Alpha systems have a rapid partition application built into the
AlphaBIOS and older Alpha Systems must make use of ARCINST.EXE. If your
machine has AlphaBIOS (MULTIA does not), just go to the partition disks
section in the menus and follow the directions there.

For those with an ARC console, you must select the menu option to run a
program and type, cd:\alpha\arcinst and press return. This assumes you
have the Windows NT CD-ROM inserted into the CD-ROM disk drive.

When you parition the disk, it is best to make the first partition a size
equal to, TotalDiskSize - 10MB. Then you make the second partition equal
to the remainder of the disk, 10MB. After you make the second partition,
delete the first partition, and then go back again and make the second
partition a SYSTEM PARTITION.

When you install Windows NT from CD-ROM, OSLOADER.EXE and the proper HAL
code for the Alpha will be loaded into the second partition. This small
10MB partition must always remain as FAT.

During the install of Windows NT, you will have the opportunity to create
a partition on the first chunk of the disk and also to format it as NTFS.
Infact, it gets formatted as FAT and later during the installation of
Windows NT it get's converted to NTFS. If the partition does not convert,
then the ARC still has it defined as a SYSTEM PARTITION, and as such,
you'll have to do the install again.

So the reason I took you through the partitioning incantations is to
ensure that the ARC does not recognize the large pportion of the disk as a
SYSTEM PARTITION, which would prevent you from making it NTFS.

If your SCSI card is not natively supported by Windows NT, and yet you
have a Windows NT driver for it on floppy, such as an IntraServer card,
when the Welcome to Windows NT Installation screen appears, the point in
time at the beginning of the istallation when the screen turns blue, you
have a two second window, during which you can press Function Key F6, and
shortly thereafter, the installation program will prompt you for the
driver.

I hope this info. helps yourself and others.


rtt



