Dave Weatherall wrote:

> Absolutely correct. The $ character is one of the non alaphabetic
> characters which is part of the Radix-50 character set. Rad-50 is a
> trick used to pack three characters into one 16-bit word. RSX-11m
> kernel and system routines used '$' and '.'. in their names to
> guarantee a non-conflicting namespace.
>

I'd forgotten about Radix-50, which featured in RT11 too. Since diskspace was at
such a premium back in those days, Rad50 came in very handy and didn't need much cpu
to process either.

