On Mon, 22 Jun 1998 13:12:57, "Brian Steele" <steele_b@spiceisle.com> 
wrote:

> >Even if NT were stable and
> >scalable, VMS would have the advantage of its text, rather than GUI,
> >orientation, which allows for (a) open access, (b) remote management,
> >(c) automation.
> 
> 
> More nonsense.  Read about NT Perl (free) and other scripting languages that
> can be used with NT.  And with utilities like NT's Server Manager, remote
> management of the NT box via a text window is moot.  And there's always
> CMD.EXE :-).

Well I won't say Nonsense :-) but the first statement about Perl 
misses the point and is a common statement when people defend UNIX 
against VMS 's DCL and EVE etc. You can run Perl with NT (and VMS) but
it is not part of NT. DCL is part of VMS, it ain't somthing you have 
to equip yourself with afterwards to enable you to do some work. In 
the same way, EMACS is NOT Unix.

I share your smiley about CMD.EXE but it is much better than the 
MS-DOS version which in turn was no where near as good as DR-DOS.

Cheers - Dave.



