Alfred Loo <alloo@NOSPAM.bigfoot.com> wrote:


>> What about the GL package itself?  Who currently owns the source code?
>> Would acquiring it and fixing it be at all feasible?  Or how about
>>
>
>The vendor owns the source code.  Acquiring it will take longer than the 1.5
>yrs to Y2K.
>
>> reverse engineering a Y2K compatible "work alike" for the GL package?
>>
>
>Same as above.

>  There are messages on this thread asking about violation of dates.  Since
>it is a financial application, date is critical right?  I was thinking of
>getting the interface to convert the dates to current dates for reporting
>purposes, eg transmission of trail balance to HQ etc.
>
> The user experience is another interesting situation.  Each entry passed
>must be dated in the 1970s.
>
>I agree this solution is a kludge.  We are in progress of putting in an ERP
>system.  This kludge and unprofessional solution is expected last a maximum
>of 2 years.
>

One approach that I have mulled over in my mind a few times but have
not seen specifically discussed, would be to create two parallel data
file sets, one Y2K compliant and one "as is" and in your case with a
28 year date setback.

The trick would be to keep these two file sets constantly
synchronized, which would require trapping all updates to each file
set, converting the dates to be compatable with the other file set,
and then sending them as input transactions to update the other file
set.  Of course you would have to ensure that these synchronizing
transactions bypassed the update trapping program.

Then as individual programs were modified for Y2K they could be
switched from the "as is" to the compliant file set.  In the meantime,
the unmodified programs could continue to be used, even past Y2K if
necessary and if dates were only cosmetic rather than critical to them
specifically.

I don't have the detailed skills to assess this approach for
practicality in a real world scenario, but it just might be worth
considering in your case.


