Subject: PWS 500au, how much L3 cache?
Date: 1998/06/26
Message-ID: <6n0b8p$bd5$1@fddinewz.oit.unc.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 366321908
Distribution: usa
Organization: Dept. of Radiation Oncology, UNC-CH
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


How do you determine how much L3 (off chip) cache is in
a Digital Personal Workstation 500au?

We ordered one of these machines supposedly with 4Meg L3 but
I think it came with no L3. The reasons I think this are:

1) Upon booting up Digital Unix 4.0d it reports: "no B-cache detected"
during the boot process.

2) Upon opening the case and looking at the motherboard I located
the cpu (under its heat sink) in the upper left corner and next to
it (between the cpu and the 6 DIMM sockets) were four small vertically
oriented sockets (thin slots) that were empty.

3) I wrote a small benchmark that allocates a variable sized array
and then sums up the entries in that array many times. Varying the
size of the array from 1Kbyte to 32Mbyte resulted in some interesting
times. from 1K to about 64Kbytes didn't affect the array access
time significantly, but from 64K to about 200K the access time
increased about 50%, from 200K to 32M the time stayed constant
again. I would attribute the rise in time from 64K to 200K as
relating to the on chip L2 (96Kbyte) being swamped. After 200K
it seems as though the access time being constant indicates that
I am limited by the main memory speed.  If I had a 4M L3 I would
have expected a change in access time somewhere between array
sizes of 1M and 8M, but it was constant.



So, am I correct in determining that this machine has no L3
currently installed? Am I correct in assuming that those 4 small
vertical slots between the cpu and the DIMMS are for plugging
in the L3 cache boards, or is the L3 somewhere else (like maybe
soldered onto the motherboard)?


-- 

   Tim Cullip
   cullip@radonc.unc.edu




