Organization: neuron, technology consultants
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Handtuning OpenVMS is still the best way to maximize system performance
over time. IF an installation is fairly stable in it's work environment,
then I can usually get it humming real good in a few short weeks.

On the other hand, automatic tuning like it's done in Windows NT, menas
that no matter what the workload, the system is always out of tune, trying
to get tuned and then the workload changes and it's all messaed up again.
Unfortunately, with Windows NT, you can't say, "There, that's it and lock
it down", it's always all over the map, unless some specific application
is real busy with the machine and then it get's maybe, 15% off optimal.
With OpenVMS, I can usually get close to 5% off optimal.

rtt



