	I don't think so for several reasons.

		1) NT only has to match or exceed UNIX in scalability.

			Since both are pursuing a shared nothing paradigm, they
			are both collectively racing towards the same goal.

		2) If shared everything becomes the NT clustering goal, hardware
		   becomes a big issue.

			Digital Engineering has the luxury of approving
			hardware configurations.  As Glenn Everhart has
			pointed out more than once, a VMS cluster is *hard*
			on disk drives.  Imagine Microsoft approving disk
			drives.

		3) The die is cast:

		http://www.tandem.com/articles/cluwntal/cluwntal.htm

"Distributed Lock Managers (DLMs) are typically employed as arbitrators to
serialize the requests and negotiate contentions. This works fine, up to a
point. If you are clustering for scalability, each new node adds additional
overhead as more CPUs contend for the shared storage resources meaning increased
DLM traffic to clutter the network and lengthening queues of serialized
requests. These problems can be postponed with high-speed interconnects, but
eventually, the DLM is a bottleneck. To avoid the inevitable throughput
degradation, the shared-nothing model is a better choice for clusters that need
more than modest scalability."


