well, there are fundamental differences in the philosophies of UNIX
versus VMS versus Winblows versus whatever. Unix is and has been
a ssytem made _by_ people who know what they're doing _for_ people
who knwo what they're doing. If you _dont_ knwo what you're doing,
you stand a chance of getting hosed. Is this necessarily bad? not if
you knwo what you're doing and wish to dispense with the protection and
get right down to business! 
On the other hand, if youre the kind of person who's always afraid of 
screwing up at something or not entirely sure you knwo what you're doing, 
then a philosophy which emphasises confirmations, protections, and so
on, in the interface and in the way one uses it, is probably the thing
for you. 
It depends on what the person wants. For me, Unix is the way to go. 
I have encountered siutations where somehting like VMS would be better
than Unix for a certain task, and encountered places where something
simplistic like DOS or CP/M would be best, thoguh ive never really seen
anythign where i could really honeslty say that windows (any version) was 
the best wayu to do something, or even close. :)


>Personally, I prefer the "sanity checking" VMS provides due to its verbosity
>(compared to unix) and english language like sematics. None of this "did I really
>mean -r or should it have been -R nonsense".

 but that only matters if (as above) i am not really sure. if i know what
i'm doing, then that 'did i realy mean -r or -R' will never come to mind. 
>
>I've often wondered how long it would take to trash a unix system by
>firing line noise into a root session :-).

well, certainly not as long as it might for the line noise to generate
SYSDESTROY /TOTAL /YES_I_MEAN_IT /TRASHIT /NOTRIGHTNOW /INMINUTES=5
or something *grin*
but dammit if i want to trash my system i dont want to have to go through
all that crap and typing to do it! 
there is nobody holding your hand with unix- which i like. systems that
try to hold your hand annoy me. 
(including some of these newer commercial unices with the 'easy to use' 
