Nick Leporacci, Jr. wrote:
> I keep hearing that by setting the clock back by 28 years, you've patched
> the Y2K problem. I don't get it. I know that the calendar year repeats the
> dates of the year every 28 years, but how does setting the year to 1972 put
> a patch on the problem. '72 isn't even close to '00.

What happends is that you modify the system to use T-28 year internally
and in all input and output functions subtract/add 28 years, so that
it for the user looks rigth, but internally can continue to use
2 digit years.

It will often by easier to rewrite the input/output functions than
to change the database record format, becaus eof conversions etc..

But it i still an ugly kludge and it is not fixing the Y2K problem
the rigth way. In a standard situation, then I would consider it a
very unprofessional solution. There may be exceptions, like if a system
is scheduled for replacement 01-jul-2000, then it may be optimal to
minimize the cost of the Y2K port even though the result is a kludge.

Arne



