>On a recent Charlie Rose interviw (on the USA PBS TV
network). Eckhart
>Pfeiffer said that he saw two major system in the future:
NT and UNIX.
I remember my perception of UNIX in 1987 (I was VMS
programmer back then). I hated DCL; UNIX shell and all those
utilities looked extremely nice. Also, UNIX API seemed
cleaner: beauty of fork and exec, powerful ipcs, file io
model where everything was just a stream of bytes. Plus C
language in UNIX; we used FORTRAN for systems programming in
VMS; C was available but we didn't use it.
I have no idea how VMS changed after 1988; I guess
compatibility issues forced DEC to maintain old APIs.
Also, we had to hand-tune VAXes for good performance. Do you
still have to do it with modern OpenVMS?

With NT we also have bloated APIs (mostly because of
necessity to maintain compatibility with old software).
However, new APIs are very neat: eg. in NT you can use
single system call to wait for multiple events: file io
completion, socket io, semaphore release, thread or child
process termination, different timers, etc. And current UNIX
lacks clean APIs structure any more because multiple
features were added gradually.

Boris





