In article <slrn6ol4kq.2mp.jmaynard@thebrain.conmicro.com>, jmaynard@thebrain.conmicro.com (Jay Maynard) writes:
>On 19 Jun 1998 16:10:52 GMT, D.Webb <david20@alpha2.mdx.ac.uk> wrote:
>>18) Meaningful English like command language (DCL) and comprehensive and useful
>>    online hierachical HELP system.
>
>While I agree that VMS seems to be more a bulletproof production OS, and
>many of your points are at least arguably true from that standpoint, one
>objection from this quarter: I'm getting COBOL fingers. DCL represents a
>three- or four-times increase in the nuber of keystrokes required to get
>things done at the command line. Besides that, why did they pick $ as a
>common character? At least they could have chosen something that wasn't
>shifted.
>

There are clearly two different design goals at work here:

1.  minimize keystrokes to facilitate interactive work
2.  maximize descriptiveness so that procedures are more understandable,
    and hence maintainable, by "those who come after".


As others have noted, you can address the first half of this by defining
your own set of symbols, or shortening everything down to the minimum 
unique command length.

Your phrase COBOL fingers is also correct though, since that language
also had the 2nd design goal and so is also very verbose.  As a consequence
of the DCL interface's generally verbose style it is usually a whole lot
easier to make sense of DCL procedures than sh,csh,perl etc., especially
when dealing with unfamiliar programs.  In this instance it is better to
write out the full names and qualifiers, rather than to worry about saving
a bit of time when typing them in, or worrying about having to hit shift to
get the $ symbol. 

Ie, one line from a procedure:

   $ PROCEXAFOOBAR -
     /REMOVE_DUPLICATES -
     /UPPERCASE -
     /TRUNCATE_LINE=255 -
     somefile.txt someresult.txt

vs.

   % Procexafooobar -R -u -t255 somefile.txt someresult.txt

In neither case do we know what procaptfoobar does, but at least we can 
make an educated guess about what some of the command line options do
for the DCL case.  Often the limited amount of information is a big help
in figuring out what to modify, and what not to modify, when dealing with
inherited procedures.  

Also, note the gratuitous and arbitrary use of cases in the latter
command, wherease in DCL we need not ever worry about whether /Uppercase,
/UPPERCASE, or /uppercase mean different things, or if one form will work,
and the others not.

Regards,

