In discussing the qualities of VMS, I'm amazed no one has mentioned its CLI
interface.  Any application under VMS can

1.  get the raw command line and process it any way it wants, just like
    UNIX, or

2.  have DCL's built-in CLI services parse the command line for syntactic
    correctness, completeness, and consistency, knowing that by the time
    the application sees the parameters, any parameter supposed to be a
    date will actually be a valid date, filenames will be valid filenames,
    numbers really numbers, etc.  And that all required parameters are
    supplied and consistent with one another as used.

(2) is lacking in UNIX, which is getopt's raison d'etre.  But there's
nothing in UNIX that compares to VMS's CLI interface -- and VMS has the
service built in, standard, for any application that wants it.

Personally I don't buy all the guff about how VMS commands are so much more
comprehensible than UNIX commands: they're both purest jargon.  Ideally the
user seldom sees them anyhow, and the developers and system maintainers of
either system quickly learn the names and combinations they need to know.

And since I'm writing from outside the USA: what's so good about having a
command language look like English, anyway?  "Ce qui se laisse dire
s'enonce clairement, et les mots pour le dire viennent aisement."  To
anyone with the right training.

___Pete



