>As to hating DCL and thinking that the UNIX API is
cleaner - you are the first
>person who claims to have moved from VMS to Unix who I have
ever come across
>with this opinion.
Well, I still prefer UNIX shell programming to DCL (and to
MS batch language).

>
>Most of what you like in WNT is a direct pinch from VMS
with the names
>changed to protect the innocent/guilty.
>
>You have had SYS$SYNCH in VMS since god knows when probably
version 1.0.
I didn't know.

>
>Unfortunately the implementation of the API has been
compromised in order to
>accommodate backwards compatibility with Windows 3.11.
>In particular there is no consistent returning of
success/error status codes
>from calls. Also string variables are passed as null
terminated strings leading
>to buffer overflow problems.
>Also whereas with VMS a single function is heavily
overloaded with similar
>tasks by passing different variables with Windows NT you
have myriads of
>separate functions. This makes finding the required
function extremely
>time consuming in WNT.
Agreed.

10+ years ago VMS development tools were excellent: powerful
editors, symbolic debugger, excellent compilers. At that
time UNIX tools lagged behind: adb and sdb were much worse
than VMS debugger.
Probably the biggest problem of VMS was its dependency on
VAX hardware; UNIX ran on PCs and just about everything even
10 years ago.

In general it seems such a waste: good software gets
outdated too early; duplicate efforts are spent on doing
similar things over and over again.

Boris





