        Ah yes, I forgot that _all_ that the SYNONYM file(s) did for you
    was to allow you to  truncate  the  command name.  There was nothing
    like  a  symbol or alias which would give a short form of a  command
    plus "options".  In order to get the equivalent functionallity,  one
    would  often  resort  to  REXX  exec files...it all comes back to me
    now...  :-}

[...]
> Okkay...I've defined a few system logicals for things like my local binaries
> directory and so on, and followed the DEC pattern: LOCAL$DISK, LOCAL$IRC,...
> Maybe I should rethink that.
> FWIW, though, nobody's been able to figure out how to reference my
> non-clustered DSSI disk as other than $1$DIA0: or RF31$DIA0:, and at least
> one guy (Hi, Javier!) poked at my system himself... I think I'm just gonna
> define a logical for DIA0 and be done with it. (Especially if I manage to
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> acquire another disk or two.)

        Exactly.  The  default  for  disks  where  there's  a controller
    involved  (e.g.,  CI or DSSI connected) is for the _device_ to  have
    the controller's allocation class, or  nodename,  prepended  to  the
    device  string.   One of the first rules of VMS system management is
    to _not_ use the device name in procedures.  In fact, the only place
    a device name should appear  is  in  your startup procedures that do
    the actual MOUNT of the device.  The MOUNT command creates a logical
    name  (/SYSTEM/EXEC) for the device which defaults to  DISK$<label>.
    You have a choice of overriding that naming convention by  supplying
    the logical you'd like to have created on the MOUNT command line, or
    by  simply defining another logical name (a shorter version, say) in
    _addition_ to the one MOUNT creates.   We do this for our frequently
    accessed disks, e.g., the user disks are UDSLC1 and UDSLC2, etc.

        The idea of  the  logical  names,  especially  for  disks, is to
    insolate your users (and yourself!) from underlying hardware changes
    that  may change the device name.  For example, you may upgrade from
    a 1.9GB RF73 to a 4.3GB RZ29 and the device name  will  change  from
    $1$DIA0  to  $5$DUA6  (if  hosted  on an HSJ50), or to $6$DKB100 (if
    hosted on an HSZ50), etc.  But  if  you  do an image backup from the
    old device to the new, the volume label stays the same, and you only
    need  to make the edit of $1$DIA0 to $6$DKA100 in a _single_  place:
    where you do the MOUNT at  system  startup.   You  users  (and  your
    various command procedures) will never know the difference.

        [I will admit to making process logcial names for most of my DUA
    devices because I get tired of  typing $255$DUAxx rather than DUAxx,
    but  I'm  the system manager and I have reasons to deal with  device
    names frequently that most users don't.]

        -Ken
