OK. I'll toss in my few bits here, based on some 20+ years in this
business, 8 or 9 of which involved managing a small VMS cluster.

1. Security - VMS was designed for security from the ground up. That's not
to say it hasn't had or doesn't have any holes, but it has a MUCH firmer
basis on which to build than any other OS I've ever worked with or heard
about. A friend of mine who does secure systems for international banking
systems swears by VMS, not at it.

2. Ease of use - while not totally consistent, the VMS command structure
sure beats the current competition. DCL, for all its shortcomings, is still
very powerful and easy-to-learn. In addition, there's Pearl for those so
inclined. Through in the VMS HELP system and you've got a real winner.
(Yes, Windows NT has MS Help, too, but it's CONTENT that counts, not glitzy
hypertext links and screwball "wizards".) And if you want point-and-click,
I didn't think Motif was all that bad - it's just that I never really
thought Digital had their hearts in it.

3. Manageability - one of the main things I always liked about VMS was that
there was a lot of consistency from version to version in how things worked
and where all the pieces were placed. A colleague of mine was once tasked
with "packaging" several flavors of Un*x workstations for use in an AFS
network environment, and creating and maintaining a consistent system
configuration was a continual nightmare. Not only did the various Unices
differ widely but version-to-version changes were often even worse.

Then there are the tools like ANALYZE and MONITOR whose existence often
meant the difference between life and death...:-)

4. Robustness - as others on this newsgroup have often stated, given a
reasonably competent system setup - not too hard if you deign to read the
docs BEFORE you do the job - VMS can run as long as you've got power and
disk space for your system logs. It's not at all unusual to have systems
run continuously between VMS upgrades, which could be for a year or more.
Very few OSes out there can make this claim, much less actually demonstrate
it. We put our 2-node cluster on an UPS that had a half-hour reserve time
and slept soundly at night knowing that even if the power went down longer
than that, the UPS management software would do a controlled shutdown.

5. A file system that understands that the world of bits is much more than
just a stream of bytes. 'Nuff said here.

Well, these are off the top of my head, so I'm sure there a few other
positive things I've missed. Other more experienced hands will almost
certainly add to the list, however. Too bad that for most bean counters
their myopia prevents them from understanding or properly valuing usablity
when they approve computer purchases.

Peace.
