In article <6mee1a$dbe$1@andros.cygnus.com>,
Bill Moyer <billm@cygnus.com> wrote:
: ...
:   I will gather the (really good!) information people have posted to 
: this thread, summarize it, and posit a plan of action once more people 
: have had their say, but I wanted to interject to say something here:  
: This is *not* a "my OS is better than your OS" thread.  I happen to 
: like Unix...
:
I don't think there is any question of VMS replacing UNIX at this late
date, even if a free PC-based VMS appeared.  The PC is not the right
architecture for a VMS-like operating system.  The PC is inherently
fractured, limited, insecure, a moving target, a commodity, totally out of
control.  The concept that the one-and-only serious operating system of the
21st Century (NT) should be based on the PC architecture is more than a
little scary.

VMS provides a serious, stable, and scalable alternative to NT (as do
several other surviving but less well-known systems such as VOS and Tandem
Nonstop, the latter now also owned by Compaq).  Even if NT were stable and
scalable, VMS would have the advantage of its text, rather than GUI,
orientation, which allows for (a) open access, (b) remote management,
(c) automation.

VMS also has another benefit not to be taken lightly: APIs that are
documented.

- Frank



