jmaynard@thebrain.conmicro.com (Jay Maynard) writes...
}On 19 Jun 1998 17:31:21 GMT, D.Webb <david20@alpha2.mdx.ac.uk> wrote:
}>Ability to abbreviate commands to shortest unique string - for all standard
}>VMS commands this is a maximum of 4 characters.
} 
}What about the 417 qualifiers and/or options and/or parameters (anything you
}have to stick a / in front of)?

They too can be abbreviated to the minimual unique string. Instead of
"directory/before=yesterday" you can use "dir/be=y". How many keystrokes
would it take to get a listing of all files created before yesterday in your
current directory on your unix system?  OK, the existance of the "yesterday",
"today", and "tomorrow" keywords (or "y", "tod", and "tom" in minimal
abbreviation form) for dates in DCL makes this a bit shorter than it
would be for any arbitrary date. On the other hand you can also abbreviate
dates ad do a "dir/sin=1-jan" and get all files creaed since 1-jan of the
current year, sinc ethe current year is the default; likewise you can do
a "dir/sin" to get all files created today since today is the default date.

A "/" is no more keystrokes than a "-" in front of a qualifier, and DCL
has the advantage that you don't have to put a space between the command
and the qualifier. You can do a "dir/sin", but can you do a "ls-a"?

It's pretty rare that you need to use more than a couple of qualifiers on
a single command. Often you don't need to use any. If you find yourself using
a bunch all the time then you can always define a symbol that includes them
so you don't have to. If you almost always use the "/confirm" (or "/co" when
abbreviated) qualifier on your "delete" commands, then put a
"del :== delete/confirm" in your login.com file and just use "del" when you
wnat to confirm and "dele" when you don't.

Since VMS doesn't have a single root for a file system spanning all the disks,
that can make cross-disk operations have a higher keystroke count since you
have to type in the disk name for things not on the current default disk (which
is where most of the "$" characters come in - although that is entirely
avoidable by using logical names for the disks that dont have "$" characters
in them). Of course, you can always define a 1 character rooted logical name
that points to all of your disks, which is them somewhat like a single root
for the entire filesystem for some purposes.

--- Carl



