Sorry bad phrasing. I did not mean to imply that you were forced to
use DCL$PATH only that if you did use it you then had to specify the
full filename.

As to backwards compatibility this is a major feature.
When you upgrade the VMS operating system not only are all commands and
features backwards compatible but so are all your user written programs.
I will qualify this slightly -  all your normal programs. If you
want to write programs which play around with internal data structures
in non-standard ways then given the privileges you can but then you may
be caught out by changes in the operating system.
But the way that VMS shareable libraries ( equivalent to WNT DLLs )
work means that properly written programs will continue to run with no
need for relinking. 
ie A program created on VMS 4.x, VMS 5.x or VMS 6.x will just need to be
copied to a VMS 7.x system and run. ( This does not of course apply to
moving between architectures ie VAX <-> Alpha ).

Also the latest versions of VMS can still be run on very very old 
VAX hardware unlike some Unix manufacturers who force you to buy new
kit every few years since the latest version of the operating system
is incompatible with that hardware.
I haven't the opportunity to try it not having the hardware but I would
be surprised if VMS 7.1 would not run perfectly (given the right amount
of memory and system disk space ) on the Original VAX 780 - released in
1977.
This hardware backwards compatibility together with clustering allows
companies using VMS to dispose of systems when they want to rather than 
at the dictate of the system supplier. Old Vaxes never die they just
get clustered.


David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
Middlesex University



