Ramon L. Tate wrote:
> 
> In article <6mee1a$dbe$1@andros.cygnus.com>, billm@cygnus.com (Bill Moyer)
> wrote:
> 
> 4. Robustness - as others on this newsgroup have often stated, given a
> reasonably competent system setup - not too hard if you deign to read the
> docs BEFORE you do the job - VMS can run as long as you've got power and
> disk space for your system logs. It's not at all unusual to have systems
> run continuously between VMS upgrades, which could be for a year or more.

Also if by up you mean available to users then using a cluster and a
shadowed system disk you can even have your cluster stay up through an
operating system upgrade.

To do this you simply :-

1) Split shadowed system disk. The systems will still be running from a 
   reduced member shadow disk.
2) Take down one of the systems - leaving the other system up and   
available to users. 
3) On the downed system do any firmware upgrades if this is an Alpha.
4) Upgrade operating system for downed system using disk you removed
from shadowset as your system disk.
5) Reboot upgraded system using this new system disk.
6) Take down next system - note even if you only have two systems your
   users have the system you just upgraded available to use.
7) Upgrade firmware if alpha system and boot using new system disk.
8) Repeat steps 6 and 7 for all cluster members

(The above assumes a homogeneous cluster with a single shadowed system
 disk - other mutiple system disk configurations are possible which can
 be handled by a slight modification of the above procedure ).

Since you can also have mixed version clusters (Alpha and VAX) you can
even use this technique of adding and removing cluster members to
perform a transition between architectures whilst still maintaining 
availability to users.

David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
Middlesex University



