3400 does have embedded DSSI bus? I think so but then again,
some models in the 3X00 series did have embedded DSSI and others
had KFQSA on the Qbus. Devices behind KFQSA would show up as DUxx
in VMS though.

So, you have selected the DSSI id for the device and the selected
id (3) is not used by any other device.

You probably have the disk showing host same unit number as is the 
DSSI id. At the ISE level the relevant parameters are UNIT_NUM and
FORCE_UNI (I think but cannot verify).

So, on the console level, if you do >>> SHOW DSSI, you would this
device as DIA3 at bus 0 at id 3 (system not printing it that way
though). SHOW DSSI should also tell the "ISE hostname" so maybe
you have a conflict there. For some people have been changing
the factory assigned (almost surely) unique host names to thinks
like DISK0, DISK1 etc. Check that for all you devices. DSSI ISE
level parameter is SCSNODE.

Boot VMS, pay attention the first seconds/minutes of system coming
up. Does VMS complain about "conflicting SCSNODE/SCSYSTEMID" pair?
Unable to actually form circuit to mentioned ISE. Check SCSNODE,
SCSSYSTEMID, ALLCLASS settings for all disk using

$ set host/dup/server=mscp$dup/task=params -
'host_name_of_disk_as_seen_in_the_SHOW_CLUSTER'

(Again, I am at home and cannot absolutely surely verify these
commands.)


Do you have configure process running? try

$ @sys$system:startup configure

maybe the device will show up in $SHOW DEVICE D or you might get
some revealing error message from VMS. (Please do everything from
console terminal or at least make sure you can receive operator
messages).

And last, you do have the disk RUN button (or whatever it is) at
approriarite position?

