Oh, come off of it.  You may not want to use MS stuff - hell knows, *I*
don't - but that doesn't change the facts.  The bulk of new development
- both commercial and share- or free-ware - is for MS.  Yes, I know all
about the Gnu stuff, and Linux, and so on.  These can be wonderful
programs, but they're for programmers, not for end-users - and for the
most part the code that's being developed hasn't gotten better visual
interfaces than you could find 10 years ago.  I use tgif for editing
graphics on Unix systems, and it's not a bad program - but Visio it's
not.

Ten, 15 years ago, I used VMS and lamented the huge volume of Unix
programs that didn't work on it.  (I re-wrote and ported a number of
them of the years.)  These days, I use both VMS (less all the time,
sadly) and Unix.  Porting the Unix stuff to VMS has become easy.  But
now I keep seeing things that run on Windows, but nowhere else.  If the
old Unix to VMS ports were sometimes messy, the Windows-to-anything
ports are just plain impossible in almost all interesting cases.  (VMS
could have jumped ahead of Unix on this front if DEC had wanted the
much-vaunted "Integration" effort to go in that direction, but that's a
dead issue now.)

Hell, even Sun recognizes this reality:  Pretty much from the beginning,
*Sun's* Java implementations have worked *much* better on Windows than
on Solaris!  (To this day, there are really no Unix Java implementations
that compete with the best Windows implementations.)

I wish, I really wish, it weren't so.  But I don't respond by insulting
those who recognize the sad reality.
							-- Jerry



