There are a lot of discussions on whether porting VMS to the 8086 would have
saved DEC.

Am I not right in saying that prior to the announcement that Aplha was in the
works, the 8086 family was not yet powerful enough to be taken seriously ?

And would I not be right in saying that by the time the 8086 became serious
enough to run VMS, Digital was already busy porting VMS to Alpha (or preparing
to port to it) ?


Once Alpha, a much more powerful chip was out, it would have cost DEC a lot
less to just lower their ALPHA workstation prices (the ones that allowed VMS
to boot) to be competitive to PCs instead of ensuring that ALPHA boxes were
always slightly more expensive than DEC's own PCs which were also always a bit
more expensive that street prices.

Alpha's viability was only "officially" questioned when Digital sold its FAB
plant to Intel.
Prior to that, DEC maintained that ALPHA would be succesful. 

Had Digital acknowledged ALPHA's lack of commercial viability earlier, it
would then had made sense to port VMS to PCs. But if you want your chip to
succeed, you don't go out and port your operating system to the cheap competition.



