From: aceware@iinet.net.au (Tony Epton)
Subject: Re: Hints on transporting a 780...
Date: 1998/06/18
Message-ID: <358862c1.255753625@news.m.iinet.net.au>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 364833176
References: <3588C209.6ED71FEC@tec.puv.fi>
Organization: Aceware Programming Pty Ltd
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I have had limited experience with dismantling, moving and
reassembling a 780.
(limited to the extent that I have not attempted to get the thing
working yet)
The obvious thing of course is copious labelling on all connection
points and leads you dettach.
I completely removed all the power supply modules and back planes
and even a large amount of the metal work. This reduced the weight to
the point where I could  load the cabinet onto a small truck on my
own. (approx 8 hours disassembly time)
We are not talking about rolling here - more - tilt cabinet. grab base
and lift !
The only gotcha I can warn you about is that you only have to breath
on any of the back planes and you will bend pins. It seems that there
is almost no way to carry the back plane / card cage assemblies
without pins hooking in to your clothing and subsequently bending.
Also they are quite heavy and awkward to carry if you leave the cards
in (which I would still recommend).
I am also told that the back plane interconnects are very touchy and
that once you disturb them they are difficult to get working reliably
again.
I am sure the more experienced people will throw their hands up in
horror at the process I have described - but still - it might lead to
some more useful warnings

Tony Epton
Curator - Australian Computer Museum Society (WA branch)



