The MIPS OSF/1 nonsense is pretty hard to regard as anything but deliberate
dishonesty on DEC's part.  For example, where I was in school at the time,
we bought new DECstations on the promise -- both from our local rep and
repeatedly echoed here on the net -- that everything would run OSF/1, that
binary recompliers would be available, and that MIPS->Alpha migration would
be something we could defer for several years without pain.

Of course, we'd also put up with several years of "fixed in OSF/1" as a
response to any serious problem we had with Ultrix, just like all the other
Ultrix customers.  Heh.

It's hard to see cancelling and then reinstating the R4000 daughtercards
*after* design was done and parts were available as exactly MIPS' fault,
either.  Maybe hopping in bed with MIPS was a bad choice, but exposing
DEC's customers to the consequences instead of absorbing them was... typical.
If one of the options involved shafting the UNIX customers, you could bet
that that'd be the one, at that time, that DEC would pick.  Like I said,
not dissimilar to the Jupiter disaster.

