[snip]
> > Of course, eventually customer backlash made them release the R4000 upgrade
> > cards they'd promised, and I do have a "developer release" OSF/1 tape which
> > will in fact install on my pmax, but... hey, dishonesty took DEC a long
> > way.  Right through the Jupiter debacle, the first iteration of RISC VMS,
> > R4000 upgrades and MIPS OSF/1, ARC workstations, you name it.
> 
> Re: R4000 upgrades and MIPS OSF/1. Twenty-twenty hindsight reveals that
> the decision to get in bed with MIPS was a bad one. MIPS shafted DEC on
> numerous occasions. They were always a day late and a dollar short with
> their hardware. The Biggest Shafting regarded the R6000. Recall that DEC
> brought out the R6000-based DECsystem 5900. Plans were in place to do a
> quad-processor implementation several months thereafter.
> 
> Then, and only then, did MIPS tell DEC: "You can't do that, CDC has an
> 18-month exclusive on the MP version of the R6000."

  That was probably just as well.  The ECL R6000 was soon eclipsed by the
  much cheaper CMOS R4000.  MIPS's own R6000 server almost killed the
  company when Bipolar Integrated Technology just couldn't get any kind
  of yield on that pig; the financial mess resulted in SGI picking up the
  pieces.  Some of the guys from BIT went on to lose another great bunch
  of other people's money chasing the bipolar micro mirage with Exponential's
  PowerPC clone.  Again, whatever marginal gain bipolar offered (arguable
  none at this stage because of power consumption vs complexity/integration
  issues) was soon overtaken by cheaper, faster, cooler CMOS devices in the
  latest processes.

  BTW, BIT was also involved in a ECL SPARC design around the same time
  but it never even materialized AFAIK.  Also, when DG jumped on the 88K
  bandwagon it promised to do an ECL 88K part; again a no show.  So
  investors should regard talk of breakthrough performance by applying
  bipolar (or maybe GaAs will the next magic word) technology to micros
  as one would perpetual motion machines or cars that runs on water  ;-)

> 
> Don'tcha just love surprises like that?
> 
> Terry Shannon
> Shannon Knows Distributed Enterprise Computing

