Arun Sharma wrote:

> Agree with most of what you said. Irrespective of what the free
> software/open source advocates say, Windows apps are better packaged,
> easier to install/configure and are in general more usable.

Whooo-eee! tell it to my registry!  In my experience, many -- most? --
Windows apps are one-way streets: they can be installed, and they may
work when installed, but they often interfere with other apps by
installing conflicting DLLs or file associations, and they are nearly
impossible to remove, typically leaving behind a maze of weird
stuff in the registry.  For instance, two very popular apps I know
(one of them an extremely famous game) forcibly installed on my PC
versions of ancillary software OLDER than what I had already put
there myself.  One of those couldn't be uninstalled, and there are
fragments of it all over the system and in the Registry.

How often, to fix application problems under UNIX, does anyone tell
you "just reinstall the operating system"?

Even if you use no bits but those from Microsoft, the distribution,
versioning, and removal of software packages is an unsolved nightmare
-- though this may not be a problem if you're a home user, not on a
network, and use nothing but Microsoft office products and never
upgrade either them or the operating system.  It's also not a problem
if you stick to a 4-function pocket calculator.

Is this a rant?  Yes it is.  I accept that there's a lot of Windows
in the world, like polluted air: you may not like it, but you still
have to breathe it.  But that doesn't mean that it's easy or good.
Let's not lose our grip on reality.

___Pete



