billm@cygnus.com (Bill Moyer) wrote:

>   My only exposure to VMS was in my college OS design classes as 
> a case study, and I didn't see anything about it that would set it 
> above Unix.  Perhaps you could educate me with a list of VMS's 
> technical advantages? 

- Clustering.
  . Cluster-wide queueing (print, job).
  . Cluster-wide locking (without a need to go through the file system).
- Logical names (and not the limited-use environment variables you'll find
  in a UNIX system).
- Shell level date/time manipulation (you won't guess this, but we
  use a Fortran program to subtract two times because you can't do
  this on the shell level in the Bourne Shell).
- ACL's (OK, some UNICES have them too, these days).
- AST's (have sufficiently privileged processes peek into other,
  running processes without disturbing them - this, BTW, doesn't
  really sum up what you can do with them).
- Handbook and course to add your own device drivers without having to
  recompile the kernel.

And that's just a (former) sysadmins idea of VMS's technical advantages.  Oh 
boy, what we could have done with the sources in a machine readable format 
and a Bliss compiler ...

I'm sure someone else can join in to add other examples.

-- 
