Security 

ie

1) Protected authorization file  - no hack with world readable password file 
and protected shadow file.

2) Password dictionary and history list to stop users reusing passwords or
using dictionary words.

3) Minimum password length limits.

4) Even possibility of accounts with two passwords ( ie need two people present
   to log into this account )

5) Comprehensive auditing and accounting.

6) Multiple levels of privilege - not just nothing and everything (ie root )

7) Images installed with privileges so that you can give a program the exact
   privileges it needs without giving them to the user and can do so in such a
   way that the user will not retain them when the program exits.
   Much safer than Unix's SUID.

8) Memory page protection levels and guard pages to cause programs to crash
   rather than executing data as code when buffers overflow. This deals with
   many of the Unix buffer overflow security holes.

9) System designed and coded with integrity checks built in to increase
   reliability. 
 
Other things 

10) Per account resource quotas with reasonable default values.

11) Per account disk quotas.

12) Calling standard -  Write different modules of your programs in different 
    programming languages , link them with the standard linker and run the 
    program.

13) Comprehensive well documented access to system internals from system
    service and runtime library routines. 

14) Comprehensive debugger.

15) File system with default being to make sure that data integrity when
    writing  to disk is as near as possible guaranteed. 

16) Record management system (RMS) able to handle lots of different file types
    and record types. Including indexed sequential files (no need to buy a
    separate BTREE package ).

17) Reliable backup command. With backups being placed in files on tape (
    called savesets ) which are individually named. This results in easy
    identification of backups for restores. No having to work out which 
    partition dump is which on the tape before doing a restore.

18) Meaningful English like command language (DCL) and comprehensive and useful
    online hierachical HELP system.

19) Meaningful error messages in standard format.

20) Ability to define and call users own help and error message files
    from within user written applications.

21) Comprehensive interprocess communication systems 
    common event flags, files, Mailboxes, Logical names, Global sections
    and  distributed lock manager.

22) Performance monitoring tools - MONITOR.

23) System parameter tuning tools, tuning with feedback from the running
    system - AUTOGEN.

All of the above come as standard with the operating system. No need to go out
and buy a system for restricting a users disk quota or a BTREE indexed file
system.

I am sure I could come up with a whole lot more but I 'll leave that to someone
else.

David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
Middlesex University





