And I would add:

* DCL - a shell environment that is intelligently designed and
intuitive, as
        opposed to the myriad cryptic and inconsistant Unix shells.
        I often compare Unix to VMS to my friends by saying that working
in
        a Unix environment is like trying to do business in an office
building
        that is constantly under construction, with scaffolding,
girders, and
        exposed wiring everywhere to trip over.  VMS, on the other hand,
has
        a much more coherant, finished feel, largely thanks to DCL.
* Security - VMS security is rarely compromised compared to Unix or NT. 
Some
        argue that this is "security through obscurity" (there was an
extended
        discussion about this on comp.os.vms a while back), but I tend
to see
        it as the result of superior design.
* 24x365 availability - neither Unix nor NT can touch VMS where
robustness
        and reliability are concernced.

Now if only DIGITAL--err, Compaq would ADVERTISE these advantages every
once in a while, instead of trumpeting NT and "industry standard
computing."

On the bright side, the Alpha processor seems to have been getting more
publicity over the past month (thanks to the delay of Merced, the
DIGITAL-Intel settlement, the Compaq acquisition, and Samsung's Alpha
Processor, Inc. subsidiary) than in the previous 5 years put
together--sometimes even positive publicity. Here's hoping that this is
a sign that the days of DIGITAL's stealth marketing techniques are
numbered.
