In article <35890914.0@ntserv.obu.edu>, "John Selph" <selphj@alpha.obu.edu> writes:
>
>This argument is just an excuse.  The only thing that was made in mind for
>VMS was VAX structure itself.  The DEC Alpha architecture is completely
>different in every way.

The Alpha was also designed to be OS neutral.  Part of which was
including the features needed for the VMS port.

And the architecures are not "different in every way".  There are many
simularities:  flat address space, n-bit multiplies and divides create
n-bit results, VAX floating point support (one could almost say PDP-11
floating point), and memory management hardware supporting page-by-page
address translation and protection, and of course the ability
(requirement on a VAX) to run little endian.  I'm sure there are others.

