In article <358AA788.4FB8@world.std.com> shannon@world.std.com writes:
>
>VMS clearly is easier to use than is Unix. DCL commands are
>English-like, making it much easier to copy, set default, print, etc.
>
>yacc, awk, grep, and the like are pretty confusing to the uninitiated.
>
>Terry Shannon
>Who does not want to precipitate a "my OS is better than your OS"
>flame-o-rama.

  I will gather the (really good!) information people have posted to 
this thread, summarize it, and posit a plan of action once more people 
have had their say, but I wanted to interject to say something here:  
This is *not* a "my OS is better than your OS" thread.  I happen to 
like Unix, but my mind is not closed on the matter.  It's got its prob-
lems, and as soon as something better is available I'll gladly start 
using it; on a similar note, if the opportunity comes up to organize 
an effort to *make* something better, I'm more than willing to take 
advantage of it.

  I don't have enough information yet to decide whether VMS really is 
that "something better" (ie, can it do everything I need my OS to do?),
but people have mentioned very useful features which Unix lacks.  It 
occurs to me that a "VMSLinux" could be branched off the Linux source 
code and remade into a VMS-alike OS without losing its Unix strengths.  
Linux is already superior to most other Unixes (ie, it's solved some 
of the problems others in this thread have pointed out as Unix's flaws), 
so incorporating the strengths of another OS which has "already been 
there" seems like a natural enough thing to do.

  At the *very* least, a DCL shell shouldn't be too difficult.  The 
rest of what I've seen seems doable, though it would require a lot of
work -- so *if* the thought of such a project doesn't fill every VMS
user's mouth with bile, several programmers would need to be willing 
to dedicate at least a few hard hours every week to the project, 
especially if we are to do it right from the offset and avoid messy
make-do hacks (which I hate as much as the next guy).

