Ravi Kochhar wrote:
> 
>    I am helping a colleague in Belgium add a new SCSI disk to an older
>    VAXstation 3100 (I think it's a model 3100/30). He purchased an HP
>    disk (model C3728-39002), capacity 2 GB. It is attached to the
>    standard internal DEC scsi controller for that VAXstn.
> 
>    The drive is recognized by the VAXstn (as seen by "show dev" at the
>    >>> promt), but, all attempts to initialize with the "INIT ..."
>    command have resulted in a "fatal drive error" message. We have
>    more or less ruled out problems due to improper termination and
>    jumper settings.
> 
>    My question is this: Should we assume that this particular model
>    drive is inherently imcompatible with a VAXstn 3100/30 ? If so,
>    can anyone recommend another brand/model of about that capacity
>    that is known to work with a VAXstn 3100/30 ?

As mentioned in other messages, your boot disk is limited to 1GB.  I
personally like the ST41200N (CDC Wren VII).  Older versions of VMS
don't have problems with this drive.  Unfortunately, it is a 5 1/4"
drive, so you need an external case.  Also, the SCSI ROMs on the 3100
don't like the Inquiry response they get, so it fails self test.  You
have to manually boot the system.  Everything works fine once you do. 
VMS has no problems with the drive, just the SCSI self test.

For data disks, you can use much larger drives.  The exact limit is
imposed by the version of VMS, but is at least 8GB.

Whether or not a particular drive works depends on how well VMS responds
to the Inquiry string.  There are patches available for some versions
which increase the number of drives supported.  In general, older and
slower is more likely to work.  The 3100/30 SCSI controller can't take
advantage of the newer features, anyways.

You don't say what version of VMS you want to use, but some versions
require that the SCSI drive be configured a certain way.  For example,
VMS 5.5-2 requires that the automatic recovery bits (ARRE & AWRE) be
cleared.  Quite often these bits are set on PC-class SCSI drives.  This
can cause the "fatal drive error" message when INITing a disk. 
Reprogramming these bits may be all that is needed to make a drive work.

(Explaining how to reprogram the SCSI mode bits is beyond the scope of
this message.)

Good luck!

