Ken Buffie wrote:
> 
> Greetings,
> 
> I recently bought an Alphastation 200 4/233 from Onsale.com.  I've added
> a 4GB Seagate Barracuda
> SCSI-2 HD and a Sony 2X SCSI CDROM as well as 128MB RAM.  I wanted to
> load in NT 4.0 as
> my OS but my model came without the ARC bios. I've downloaded the flash
> bios updater from DEC
> but as I am unfamiliar with anything but "toy" operating systems (Mac,
> Windoze, NT).  I am lost.
> 
> How do I create a bootable disk that my Alphastation can read? Can it be
> done on the AS200 even though I don't have an operating system?  I know
> of the help command and the couple of things I can do from this but was
> not able to figure out if I could make a bootable diskette.  The manual
> that came with the AS200 is limited in information.
> 
> If I drove to my nearest DEC dealer (@130 miles) would they do it
> (without charging me a ton of $)?
> Thanks for any help in advance.

I'm assuming that when you do a SHOW CONFIG it tells you that the ARC is
not loaded.  If the ARC is loaded (but not active), just do a SET OS NT
and then cycle power.

From a VMS or DUNIX system, you can create the required floppy.  You
can't do it from the AS200's console.

Another option is to load the image over the network.  If you have a
machine which can serve MOP or BOOTP downloads, you can load the
firmware that way.

Finally, you can boot the firmware CD.  If you don't have other machines
available, this may be your best bet.  I don't know how much DEC charges
for firmware CDs, but I would hope that it isn't much.

Once you get the firmware update utility running, use the command UPDATE
ARC to change to the ARC console.

PS: I would recommend that you monitor the Barracuda carefully.  We had
a 2GB Barracuda in a AS200 4/166 and decided that it was just too hot. 
The drive bays don't seem to get a whole lot of ciculation and may
overheat with such a hot drive.

Good luck!

