[...]
>                                                     ... DCL represents a
> three- or four-times increase in the nuber of keystrokes required to get
> things done at the command line.

        Only if you insist  on  typing  more  than the minimum number of
    characters  required.   That  is,  you only need  to  type  as  many
    characters as will "disambiguate" the command from another verb, and
    _never_ more than 4 characters, at least for DCL  commands  suitably
    reviewed  by  the  OVMS  Engineering  people  (I've  seen some badly
    designed verb syntaxes  for  layered  products  for OVMS from people
    outside OVMS).

        Of course, people generally  _do_  want  to "tailor" commands to
    their own personal preferences, but I would _hate_ to type, e.g.,

        DIRE/WIDTH=(FILENAME=25)/DATE/SIZE=ALL

    75 times a day, even if  shortened  to the fewest characters!  So on
    VMS, as on other O/S's, all but the newest users define short forms,
    called  "symbols"  in DCL, aliases on unix, etc.  [Even VM/CMS,  and
    probably MVS, on IBM mainframes had/has  SYNONYM  files...]  So  the
    above becomes "DR" for me via a symbol definition in my LOGIN.COM.
    As well as a _long_ list of other _personal_ "short-forms".

>                                  Besides that, why did they pick $ as a
> common character?

        I  suppose  because  it's   like   "$YSTME".   I  have  a  vague
    recollection  that  the dollar-sign was used in similar contexts  in
    other earlier DEC operating systems  (anyone  care  to  chime  in?).
    Anyway,  I don't know about "common".  The general user "shouldn't",
    in the best of all worlds,  have  to use the dollar-sign except very
    rarely.   In  all of DEC's VMS documentation it is stated that  "the
    dollar-sign is reserved to Digital", meaning, "if you don't want  to
    have  name-space  conflicts,  don't  use the `$' in procedure names,
    logical names,  or  similar".   However,  just  about  every  system
    manager  I've ever encountered, and I'm afraid I'll have to  include
    myself in this assessment, :-} follows the VMS model  when  creating
    system-wide logical names (in particular) for local facilities, etc.
    When  that  is done, the general user is "exposed".  In a "properly"
    managed system,  these  logicals  could  easily  be  defined without
    either a $ or an _, but just unshifted characters.  Oh well...

        -Ken
