Ric,

Having been with DEC in 81-86 and at Alliant for a short while...
and with Concurrent at the merger with Masscomp...

It wasn't quite what you said.  DEC's VMS was doing well in commercial
markets, but they were running away from the scientific and real time
market in favor of trying to lever commercial business from the IBM
mainframers and Sys 3x'ers.

VMS and clustering was still looking good into about 89-90... when it was
obvious DEC was running out of horsepower and the RISC platforms
were taking hold.

I don't think KO would've run DEC into chapter 11 -- but I do feel that
he let far too many of the subordinates build kingdoms and imported folks
who didn't understand DEC's main advantage -- immediate, interactive
computing.

This cost DEC.  We were being constantly lectured in the field service 
organization about profitability, inventory cost, per-person income and
productivity (in 1985 and 86).  I hoped Jack Shields would've taken over
after KO -- since he seemed to head the one customer oriented group.

(I sat in on a meeting about the profit issues that predicted a fall of
DEC in 1986 -- it used Compaq's numbers as guidelines as to what
DEC needed in revenue and income per employee to be successful with the 
lower margins of the future).

Bill
