Steve Simmons wrote:
> BINGO!!!!   In the late 80's, many of the smarter powers at DEC (me excluded)
> understood that RISC had a better long term viability than the VAX. In fact,
> Dave Cutler was driving that effort; however, nobody wanted to kill the goose that laid
> the golden eggs.  Each VAX sale had a high profit margin, and DEC built
> a cost structure to mirror this.

I understand, and to some extent agree, but I also disagree. The real reason
that DEC lost the momentum is that it failed to see the PC as competition,
while the PC maker's goals were to eat into the larger systems, and DEC, blind
to this, made the PC makers job very easy by keeping its prices so high.

Keeping the price of its VAX-C compiler in the thousands for a MVAX-II, while
MS was selling 
a more complete environment for about 1/10 the price is a good example of what
killed DEC. How can you justify to a boss software which costs so much more
than on a PC which can do about the 
same ? (from the beancounter's point of view).

People didn't leave the VAX because it wasn't risc. They left it because it
cost more per mips
(the measure back then) than thew new guys in town. Cash cow yes, but it was
also not a viable cow because it was too fat.

DEC priced itself with IBM as competitor and didn't want to admit PCs and
others were serious competition. But all the others saw DEC and IBM as the
perfect targets for new customers because thei is where their prices were the
most competitive against those of DEC and IBM.



