>There was no implicit refusal.  While the Pentium itself may have
>sufficient capability to run VMS, it can't do it while attached to a
>PC-style motherboard.  The old 8086 compatibility stuff (required to run
>MacroShaft "operating systems") lacks sufficient hardware interrupts,
>for one thing, to run VMS.  That old memory monkey business (640K,
>device registers, and then more memory) doesn't help either.  A totally
>new architecture built around the Pentium could possibly support VMS,
>but such a motherboard would no longer be a commodity, and be priced
>considerably higher.


This argument is just an excuse.  The only thing that was made in mind for
VMS was VAX structure itself.  The DEC Alpha architecture is completely
different in every way.  But DEC went through the massive port effort for
VMS and why?  Because the DEC Alpha was their baby and they wanted VMS on
it.  You can port an OS to just about anything if you really want it.  DEC
wanted to play with the big boys.  They price in IBM's range but the truth
is, they weren't IBM.  People in the midrange world consider DEC to be the
"cheap and dirty" company, like it or not.  Now that DEC is no more, it only
proves they were right.  I'm not trying to start a war.  I guess I'm just
pissed that DEC has made such a big mistake and destroyed themselves.  The
sad thing is the field rep I talked to told me they had been aware of the
buy out plans for 5 years.





