Losing the scientific computing business was a big mistake.  I was at a
Nuclear Physics conference in Pacific Grove CA in 1989 and they had
an evening session where computer vendors would get up and tell the group
about their plans for the future.  A guy from Sun got up and told about
their great new hardware, compilers and operating systems that were
going to help physicists collect and sort their data, crunch their numbers
and calculate their theories.  Digital sent some poor soul who got up and
gave some lame board-room presentation about visualization and other
stuff that CEOs like to hear.  I assume Digital's attitude towards other
scientists and academics was similar.  There are LOTS of UNIX boxes
in science and academia where there used to be DEC machines.
Digital should have stuck with its bread and butter - technicians building
and selling systems for technicians to use.

I won't  get into the thing about how science was the driving force
behind the develoment of the Internet and the World Wide Web stuff and how
if DEC hadn't abandoned the scientific community VMS might have a
commanding lead in the intenet today instead of lagging behind.

As for academia, I think the only thing that has helped Digital hang on to
what College and University business that they have left is the
CSLG/ESL program.  Right, Compaq?

Don M.

