"John Selph" <selphj@alpha.obu.edu> writes:

> This argument is just an excuse.

Well, in part...but see below.

>  The only thing that was made in mind for
> VMS was VAX structure itself.  The DEC Alpha architecture is completely
> different in every way.  But DEC went through the massive port effort for
> VMS and why?  Because the DEC Alpha was their baby and they wanted VMS on
> it.  You can port an OS to just about anything if you really want it.

Sure, but at what effort? The Alpha architecture has some serious hooks that
help porting VMS: PAL code, four access modes, support for ASTs and software
interrupts, support for the VAX FP formats, to name the main ones. VMS itself
could have done without the latter, but missing the others would have required
a very serious re-implementation, instead of a port, of central parts of the
kernel. 

	Jan



