They then of course repeated the exact same strategy of deception while
screwing over all the customers who bought their MIPS hardware.  Bang, there
went a significant piece of their UNIX workstation market share, pissed away
by lying to technical people to get sales recommendations for the MIPS boxes
which were about to be cancelled so that they could try to shorten their
life cycle in favor of new, incompatible Alpha machines instead of upgrades.

Of course, eventually customer backlash made them release the R4000 upgrade
cards they'd promised, and I do have a "developer release" OSF/1 tape which
will in fact install on my pmax, but... hey, dishonesty took DEC a long
way.  Right through the Jupiter debacle, the first iteration of RISC VMS,
R4000 upgrades and MIPS OSF/1, ARC workstations, you name it.  Took DEC
right into the arms of a two-bit Wintel vendor that almost went under itself
a few years ago.

I don't know if the habitual dishonesty to customers or the Alpha Stealth
Marketing hurt worse...

