In article <35893A3D.6A35DA63@bbc.co.uk>,
	Tim Llewellyn <tim.llewellyn@bbc.co.uk> writes:
> Those 3100s ran DECWindows
> like a dog (apart from M76 with SPX controller)

I didn't find X/Motif on a 3100/40 too objectionable, but perhaps I
was just more patient than most as I grew up on 1200bps VTs at
college, and was amazed at the huge 9600 (and the 19200)bps when I
finally started working for a living.  :)

> Now the PC's are taking over from the Unix boxes as well.

In hardware terms, perhaps, but all that's really happening is that
Unix is starting to infect the PCs that are spreading into the
workstation territory and seems not only to have taken a hold there
but is beginning to spread elsewhere in the traditional MS sphere;
probably the freebies like Linux and *BSD are helping that along
since the usual vendors would've never lowered prices enough for
domestic and small business use.

It's a shame that a concerted effort to port VMS never really
happened, it could theoretically have worked in spite of such
handicaps of only two modes of operation instead of four (some
relatively simple virtualisation could possibly have been one
way of taking care of this) and various other aspects.  OTOH,
there's still the free-VMS project out there and, whilst still
in its infancy, we may yet see some interesting results.  VMS
isn't dead yet, far from it, and if some freebie or inexpensive
version becomes available to the Great Unwashed there's always
the possibility of a resurgance[1].

Chris.

[1] When I say "available..." I mean something that runs on the
