In article <358415b4.162400025@news>, Greg.Gardner@gsc.gte.com writes:
:In maintaining a legacy VAX/VMS application, our software developers
:are unable to get past a Reserved Operand error that's cropping up.
:We're desparately looking for ideas re how to pinpoint the cause of
:the error or to find a way around it.  DEC has not been of much help
:as the system is no longer under software maintenance.

  Look at the instruction that is faulting, the code around it, and work
  backward from there.  This could be an attempt to execute data as code,
  due to a corrupted stack, a transfer of control into a data program
  section, or various other reasons.  Also carefully consider what (else)
  happened around the time that the error started appearing.

	--

 ROPRAND,  reserved operand fault at PC='location', PSL='xxxxxxxx'


  Facility:     SYSTEM, System Services

  Explanation:  An instruction contains an operand in a format that is not
                acceptable. This message indicates an exception condition and
                is usually followed by a display of the condition arguments,
                registers, and stack at the time of the exception.

  User Action:  Check for a programming error. Examine the PC and virtual
                address displayed in the message to determine the instruction
                that caused the error.


