In article <35857085.7EBB@world.std.com>,
Terry C. Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> wrote:
: ...
: Truth be told, I'd like to write a book ("DECline") on the last decade of
: Digital. Input gratefully accepted.
:
I think the decline began in 1983, so make that a decade and a half.  In
retrospect, the first -- and perhaps biggest -- nail in DEC's coffin was the
cancellation of the 36-bit line.  These were the computers upon which the
ARPAnet (Internet) was based, and from which all the well-known applications
sprang, etc etc.  The DEC-10/20 community was highly productive, innovative,
and community-spirited, and this fed back into DEC and strengthened it.

When DEC dumped this line (perhaps there were some good reasons for it), it
alienated this community and they all turned to Sun (etc), vowing never again
to depend on a proprietary platform, and eventually scattered to the wind.

And then came NT, but that's another story.

- Frank



