There was no implicit refusal.  While the Pentium itself may have
sufficient capability to run VMS, it can't do it while attached to a
PC-style motherboard.  The old 8086 compatibility stuff (required to run
MacroShaft "operating systems") lacks sufficient hardware interrupts,
for one thing, to run VMS.  That old memory monkey business (640K,
device registers, and then more memory) doesn't help either.  A totally
new architecture built around the Pentium could possibly support VMS,
but such a motherboard would no longer be a commodity, and be priced
considerably higher.

>Lack of built-in support for IP was another nail in the coffin, I
think.
>Got to spend $$$ for VMS and another set of $$$ to network it with your
IP
>boxes???


Look at most major operating systems and you'll see that network support
is layered on top of the O/S.  It may, as in most Unixes and M$ O/Ss, be
shipped with the O/S already installed.  DEC's mistake was in not
offering it sooner and for the price of existing network licenses.  As
it stands now, if you have a DECnet license (also extra cost) you can
also run UCX.

Mark Levy
System Management Associates






