billm@cygnus.com (Bill Moyer) writes:
>   This simply isn't true.  Anything that is duplicated in the universe
> can be stored once and pointed to multiple times wherever that thing
> appears.  Even the machine itself can store schematics for itself and
> use a pointer into its own physical memory and disk device(s) instead
> of actually storing a duplicate of that information (which is impossible,
> as you point out).

Let's see.  The machine's physical workings are referred to directly in
the model.  The model thus refers not just to "modelled" phenomena in
some (possibly quantum) symbolic language of the machine, but is able to
use actual physics _as_ the language, for at least this part of the
model.

Sounds like a real live juicy universe to me :-)

[ Actually I reckon the above trick, of a machine modelling its own
workings by self reference, will lead to bigger steps forward in
computing than QC.  But I digress {further ;-)} ]

>   But this is getting quite silly .. we simply cannot hope to model
> the universe to this degree of precision because the universe is not
> deterministic (ie, events on the macro level depend on events on the
> subatomic level, some of which are nondeterministic).  QC doesn't 
> solve this problem.

Depends what sort of nondeterminism.  But I do agree with you.

We don't know what magic goes on in a QC when we make big machines yet.
We can only theorise about a few bits; big QC machines make the rash
(IMO) assumption of large scale coherence.  We may get "imperfect"
coherence that has computational properties we haven't thought of.
(Complex dynamics and all that..)  A big QC using its own physics as
part of its model would seem to have an intimate connection with nature,
no?  And modelling something real would imply measuring the real thing
at first, thus coupling the physics in the machine with the real thing.
So maybe it can display interesting variations on "nondeterminism", in
the quantum sense.

-- Jamie



