Help!!

In maintaining a legacy VAX/VMS application, our software developers
are unable to get past a Reserved Operand error that's cropping up.
We're desparately looking for ideas re how to pinpoint the cause of
the error or to find a way around it.  DEC has not been of much help
as the system is no longer under software maintenance.

As an alternative, are there any consultants out there with relevant
experience and available for some short term telephone consulting?

Thanks for any help!!

Brief description:

An Ada application, with embedded SQL, accessing an Oracle database,
encounters an ROPRAND Ada error while trying  to fetch records from
the database.  The ROPRAND error causes the SQL operations to fail,
which in turn causes the application to return a fatal error status.
The application runs on a VAX 6440 with VMS V5.2, VAX Ada  V1.5-44 and
Oracle V5.1.22.

Related Information?

VAX Fortran:
     Error Name: ROPRAND
     Number: 144
     Error Code: Reserved operand
     Message Text: Digital Fortran 90 encountered a reserved operand.
Please report the problem to Digital.

VAX Debugger:
    The problem lies not in the hardware but in the OS.  When you
write-lock a page in VM, any system call that tries to update this
page will fault.  In VMS this would cause a system panic. Therefore,
the VMS debugger would copy the the system service vector and replace
it with one that jumped into the debugger code.  The debugger would
then unlock all write locked pages and flip a bit on the PSW.  The
debugger would then invoke the respective system service.  The
debugger would come back in on an exception when the system service
was complete because of that PSW bit.  This bit would cause a fault on
return from the service call because the PSW would be illegal, ROPRAND
fault.




