Hmmm.  I see the distinction you are making.  I had been thinking
`smart terminal' rather than `network computer.'  By those lights the
earliest of Bell tech were `smart terminals.'  But really smart!
Certainly the early Blit,5620 series used dedicated host connections.
Some later models had LAN and ISDN adapters.

As to how much ran locally on these terminals, here is a short list
from memory and from the FAQ:

  xcip	    drawing program
  xlens     screen magnification
  xproof    troff viewer
  xsysmon   top-like program for host
  xtek4014  guess!
  xjf       font editor

And my favorite was GEBACA, a game.  I haven't seen this in many
years.  I think it stood for `Get Back at Corporate America,' and
involved shooting at icons such as the McD golden arches.

    Peter> I wish Lucent would release Plan 9 under some kind of
    Peter> hobbyist-cost license.

Me too!  Although the Inferno downloads are free and fun to play
with.  I'd love to solve corporate problems with this technology.  The
IL/Styx technology and reconfigurable namespaces are very powerful.

