Its a nice thought, but you'd need a computer bigger than manhattan
to do that. You'd have to simulate everything at the level of
subatomic particles. Quantum physics plays a large part in those larger
scale processes. Nevermind that you can't properly predict or simulate
things at that level of scale, the amount of information in just 200 
pounds of animal material (for example a human) is staggering. 
even a crude model would require keeping track of detailed information
on roughly 2^80 particles. Even if youre only storing a byte per particle,
you do realize that that is almost a dozen orders of magnitude greater
than the biggest computers today. Probably over a billion times as
much information as can be stored in all the disk platters ever manufactured
to date. (very rough numbers here but you get the idea)

As for simulating nuclear explosions, theyre not simulated with true
multibody interactions between individual particles, but with very complicated
and precise fluid dynamics. That in turn is based on simpler many-body
codes which do silmuate things linke neutrons, but it is impossible even
for the biggest supercomputers to have a chance at simulating more than a
few particles at once. 

