"Brian R. Walsh" <walshb@pfizer.com> writes:

> Well, the deal was approved. According to BUSINESS WIRE, "As of the
> close of the stock market on June 11, 1998, Digital common stock will
> stop trading on the New York Stock Exchange." I feel like an old friend
> has passed away. I have been working with DEC computers for 23 years ot
> of my 26-year career. 

The first computer I ever really had under my control was a
PDP-8L. That was back in 1972. I had full engineering drawings and
"gate-chased" the entire logic. (It was really a RISC system, but
nobody had invented the term.)

When I went out into the real world my first job was supporting a
DCC-D112, a REAL cheap copy of a PDP-8. I kept it working for about a
year while building a tape drive interface and writing software for
same. (Not really a driver as we didn't run an OS, just stand-alone
programs.)

From there it was on to two more DEC systems...a PDP-11/40 and a
PDP-1. The 1 was one of the first three computers DEC ever built. I'm
told that Ken O. personally supervised the installation. He may have been
a poor fortune teller, but he knew how to engineer a computer!

I'm entering this on a 500 MHz Alpha that sits under my desk and has
more compute power that the entire computer center at Lawrence
Livermore National Laboratory when I started there. And it was the
biggest computer center in the world (except for No Such Agency which
was a huge unknown).

I've come a long way with DEC, and it's very sad to see it go, but the 
DEC of today was a sad shadow of the DEC of 20 or even 10 years ago. I 
only hope the Compaq can carry on a bit of the engineering heritage of 
DEC, for that (certainly not marketing) is why I was so attached to
DEC. 

(P.S. When I finally broke down a bought a PC a few months ago, I
bought a Compaq. Guess I was stay true to my past even though I didn't 
realize it at the time.)

-- 
