Benchmarks are funny things. Most people plunking down and running them
really do not know how to analyse what they are looking at and determine
if it makes sense or not.

I once had a programmer with two terminals in front of him. He told me
that DCL was just as fast as VAX Basic. DCL is interpreted by a three pass
interpreter and VAX Basic is compiled.

He ran a DCL command procedure on one terminal, it did a write sys$output
and said Hello, in a continuous loop. On the other terminal he ran a Basic
program doing the same thing.

On his first pass, he showed me how the DCL went whipping by on the screen
compared to the slow Basic on the other terminal. I let him gloat for a
few minutes and asked him what he thought was wrong, to which ensued an
argument about how he could be wrong. I asked him to reverse the run,
Basic on the first terminal and DCL on the second and asked him to explain
the results. His first comment was that one terminal line was getting more
CPU time than the other, irregardless of the fact that both sessions were
over LAT, on the same processor and both in his account with identical
quotas. Of course, the terminal where Basic was now running was whipping
by at a high speed. After a while he gave up, suggesting that mysteries
were meant to stay that way.

Of course, the first and most rapidly running VT220 was operating in Jump
Mode for Scrolling and the second slower VT220 was operating in smooth
mode. I set the second up in Jump Mode and now he was amazed that both
program executed at what visually appeared to be the same speed. He thus
declared that an interpreted language was in fact, no faster than a
compiled language.

I left him that afternoon, calling back to him and aksing just what he
thought he was testing and that I expected a thorough answer the next
morning. I suggetsed that his next performance review may be influenced by
his understanding of exactly what he was doing.

The next morning, he cam ein with no new ideas and confessed that he had
been a little to cocky and that he truly didn't understand.

I told him he had been testing the Baud Rate of the terminals and yes,
they both produce the same visual speed of operation at 9600 baud.

Now this guy was not a dummy, although I have shown here in this instance
he was. He had a distinct lack of knowledge and no foundation for what he
was doing, and so just blindly dove in, trying to prove a point without
knowing what it was that he was doing.

I did show him how to write a time stamp into a file and then a second and
then to compare the two runs, that made him follow that he had been on the
wrong track. I showed him how to obtain the information about how much CPU
time he had used for the DCL and then for Basic.

What I am getting at here is that in any Benchmark of any kind, there are
oodles and oodles of variables, including the person running the Benchmark
and it is most important that when someone says, that the sky is green,
they better know exactly why and have a good explanation too.

I think Benchmarks in the hands of non-cognisent people are a dangerous thing.

rtt


