You got this wrong, the systems tested were the 21164 systems, based on the
SX and LX chipsets. The benchmark used was AIM which is a benchmark I must
admit I'm not familliar with. Most people who use Alpha systems will tell
you that you can get widely varying performance, depending on you code.
However most will see performance ratios much closer to those given by the
SPEC95 benchmarks (which I have hard use much more complex test programs)
than the ones given in that review.

For intance, in PC Magazine a few months back, they compared a 533SX systems
(cost about 2000 UKP) to a dual PII 300 (about 3000 UKP), and found that
software that can use both CPU's together  the Alpha still managed to
produce ~75-80% the performance of the Intel system, but for software that
was unable to use the 2 CPU's in the Intel box, the Alpha was significantly
faster.

It is a typical magazine rewiew in some ways, they test a year old system
against a machine that could be at most 3-4 months old. When the 21264
systems become available (my guess would be late August, early September) it
would be very different. The new 21264 systems have a completely different
motherboard/backplane (ie: upto 333Mhz and 128bit wide cross-bar
architecture)

Laurence

