No, it's standard 34-pin floppy. It just doesn't use the PC-standard cable 
with the conductors twisted. The pin-type connector is common, if not 
standard, on 3.5" drives.

> 4) Are there any modern, more easily acquired monitors that
> 	are compatible with this card?

I'm running mine on a Sony Multiscan 17sf in mode 4 (1152x900, (mumble) 
Hz). I would expect that any modern multisync monitor will work. The point 
is not that you have to use the particular monitor they list for each mode, 
but rather that the mode they list will work with each monitor in that 
column. If you're buying a monitor, pick the mode you want to use, then buy 
a monitor that will support the pixel clock and horizontal/vertical refresh 
frequencies they list for that mode; that should be listed in the monitor's 
specs. If you're using an existing monitor, pick any mode you want to use, 
as long as your monitor can handle the pixel clock and refresh rates listed 
for that mode.
