Ketil Z Malde wrote:
> So we throw bandwith into the bitness issue?  Does this mean that
> the Pentium is only a 2-bit processor, since the memory bus only
> can supply about two bits per clock tick?
> 
> That's certainly an interesting approach, and probably a pretty
> valid one, but unfortunatley rather original as well, I think.
> 

I was using bandwidth because it was the primary complaint that
my father seemed to have with the marketing claims made about the
68000 and 8088. He didn't seem to feel that any processor with
only a 16- or 8-bit memory data bus had a right to be called a
32- or 16-bit processor (respectively). I'm not sure I entirely
agree with him on that score (at the time I was more concerned
with the width of the internal registers and the "native" word
size of the ALU than with external bus size), but later events
have bourne out his wisdom on the matter. (CPU's marketed as
N-MHz machines when their bus interface could only supply N/2
or N/4 MHz worth of data)

Besides, the post to which I was responding had already brought
issue up. Bandwidth, for me, is just a shorthand reference for
the business of "most annoying limitation" since the bandwidth
is likely to be a good measure of the annoyance level.

I wasn't aware that the Pentium was only able to move one word
every 16 cycles on the memory bus. Surely the Pentium supports
some form of fast page mode or burst access to memory, which
should decrease the cycles per word ratio. I would expect that
the Pentium memory bus could do something more like one byte
per cycle. (but I wouldn't want to overestimate Intel's ability
to engineer crap)

- Jeff Dutky



