In article <01bd91bc$ac64ac80$LocalHost@stupidwin95>, "Kevin G. Rhoads" <T_Rhoads@NoSpam.CLASSIC.MSN.COM> writes:
>Summary:
>
>Compiler bugs are real, but should be one of the
>*last*
>things to check.
>

The best evidence that compiler bugs exist is when you submit a report to a
vendor with an example of your problem and they confirm that it is indeed a
compiler error.  In some cases they have a patch ready to send.  In others,
they say it will be fixed in the next version.  I use one Fortran 77
compiler and two Fortran 90 compilers from different vendors.  I have found
only a few bugs in the 77 compiler (not surprising since it has been around
for a long time), but I regularly find bugs in both of the 90 comiplers.
Again, I know that these are compiler bugs because the vendors have agreed
that they are.

Nevertheless, I agree with the statement that one should check every other
possibility carefully first because user errors are certainly much more
common that compiler errors.

