In article <357F78F4.15322973@shore.net> Russell Sasamori <puzzle@shore.net> writes:
>>  Regarding LFSR's, wide integers still help you here when you are
>>implementing your LFSR ADT as multiple LFSR's operating in parallel,
>>with each register representing a bitslice of all of the LFSR's.
>
>I'm trying to visualize this.  Would it help if I turned my head
>sideways?  If possible I would like to see an example in C code.

Sure .. here's an NLFSR ADT I wrote a while ago which implements eight
parallel LFSR's in a byte stream and uses multiple eight-stream LFSR's
to generate random numbers via a nonlinear function.  It's not very 
optimized code, but then when I wrote it performance wasn't an issue.

/* LFSR.H -- defining the Linear Feedback Shift Register ADT */
#include <stdtypes.h>
 
typedef struct lfsrtag {
  int32 mag;     /* number of bytes pointed to by d */
  int32 finger;  /* roving pointer into d[] to front/list */
  int32 mix;     /* bitmask of xor elements */
  byte *d;       /* data */
  } lfsrt;
 
/* LFSR.C -- defining the Linear Feedback Shift Register ADT */
#include "lfsr.h"
 
/*  crank() extracts a byte from the lfsr, performs the  */
/*  "mix" to generate the replacement byte, and returns  */
/*   the extracted byte.  */
byte crank ( lfsr ) lfsrt *lfsr; {
  byte retval;
  byte vat;
  int32 targ;
  int32 index;
  int32 mix = lfsr->mix;
 
  retval = lfsr->d[lfsr->finger];
  targ = lfsr->finger;
  lfsr->finger++;
  if ( lfsr->finger == lfsr->mag ) lfsr->finger = 0;
  vat = 0; index = 0;
  while(mix) {
    if ( mix & 1 ) {
      index = index + targ;
      if ( index >= lfsr->mag ) index -= lfsr->mag;
      if ( index >= lfsr->mag ) index -= lfsr->mag;
      vat ^= lfsr->d[index];
      }
    index++;
    mix >>= 1;
    }
  lfsr->d[targ] = vat;
  return retval;
  }
 
/*  nonlinear() returns a byte calculated by performing a nonlinear   */
/*  operation on bytes extracted from three lfsr's.  The byte stream  */
/*  obtained from nonlinear() is not suceptible to the correlation    */
/*  attack, and is a cryptographically strong source of randomness    */
/*  given that the lfsr's were initialized with random data from a    */
/*  cryptographically strong source.  */
byte nonlinear ( a, b, c ) byte a, b, c; {
  byte retval;
  byte upper;
  word32 pot;
  int  i = 0;
 
  pot = a * b;
  while ( pot && !(pot&1) ) {
    i++;
    pot >>= 1;
    }
  i++;
  pot >>= 1;
  retval = pot << i;
  if (i>=8) return c;
  if (!pot) return c;
  upper = c << (8-i);
  retval ^= upper >> (8-i);
  return retval;
  }
 
****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****
  Whether or not this NLFSR actually can be a cryptographically strong 
source of randomness is anyone's guess.  I make no claims about its 
appropriateness for any task whatsoever, despite the comments in the code.
It has been somewhat tested and appears to operate well.
****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****DISCLAIMER****

  Note - stdtypes.h is an automatically generated file.  The "stdtypes"
utility tests the host computer for various characteristics and creates 
the .h file with a bunch of standard values and types.  I use it as a 
matter of course with most of my personal projects and distribute it with 
them, to make writing portable and clearly-defined C code easier.  Feel 
free to use/promulgate it.  The source is a little large to politely dis-
tribute in a newsgroup, so I stuck it at my workplace web site:
<http://www.cygnus.com/~billm/stdtypes.c>
A sample stdtypes.h file is at:
<http://www.cygnus.com/~billm/stdtypes.h>

  -- Bill Moyer




