  Rather sad too.  I remember the undergrad days going to EE lectures
  by day and programming multitasking kernels, games, and wire frame 3D
  graphics demos (impressive stuff at the time) in PDP-11 assembly
  language at night.  My dream computer at the time was the Heathkit H-11
  - imagine having a LSI-11, a real 16 bit computer, when everyone else
  would be running 6502's and Z80's (sorry mister Olsen, I *could* think
  of what use a computer would be in the home).

  As a grad student I used VAX-11/750s and VAX-11/780s and later, micro-
  VAXes.  Later, when the Suns arrived, the VAXen seemed kind of expensive
  and slow but they were rock solid reliable old friends.  In my experience
  when you looked at piece of gear with the "digital" block letter logo you
  automatically knew 1) you definitely could have bought cheaper, 2) the
  item would be pretty reliable, and 3) it would operate as advertised.

