It took less than an hour because there was only a limited time for
'questions'.  Palmer's left hand(ed) woman stated 'we only have time for
two more 'questions''.  I did expect more of a 'fight' but Palmer had all
his ducks lined up, and the biggest problem is the only people against the
merger were the employees, who don't own more than a few % of the stock
these days.  The vote was 72.6% for, and it needed a 66.6% to pass, so the
margin was slim.  If someone took on fighting for the company (which NO ONE
DID, not even the local papers, Boston Magazine, NO ONE!) it wouldn't have
been that hard to stop this merger.  The best part of it is Palmer and the
incompetent board are out, richer by far, but out!

Palmer was asked some rather pointed questions, and he did handle him self
well, well, as well as one can be expected...he gave vague answers or
deferred the questions as inappropriate, or he claimed he just didn't know.
 In fact, he justified his big payoff by saying that when he took over
Digital, it was deshevled, and didn't have any money in the bank, which was
a flat out wrong (intentionally or not).  Digital, when Palmer took over,
had 2.5 Billion Dollars in the bank.  He even passed around a memo at that
time stating this, and comparing DEC to HP and Sun, and DEC had far more
cash in the bank then both of them.  Memory loss comes with age, and 50
Million dollars too ;-)

Oh well, apathy at its best.

