Doug Siebert (dsiebert@icaen.uiowa.edu) wrote:
: Ketil Z Malde <ketil@ii.uib.no> writes:

: >Is there *any* potential, imagined problem, where 128bit adressability
: >is necessary?  (Well, except for the internet, of course :-)


: 64 bits isn't enough to directly address all the storage in the world, so
: you need to go higher.  Maybe not to 128 bits, but you might as well because
: at some point 96 bits won't be enough.

: Why directly address all the storage in the world?  I dunno, why not?
: Someone will come up with a good reason to do it.  Some probably have
: really good reasons already.

: I don't see a compelling reason for a 128 bit CPU today, but as transistors
: get cheaper and cheaper the cost of doing it will go down to the point
: where you won't need many applications that want > 64 bits before it makes
: sense to do it.

: The Motorola "Altivec" stuff (souped up MMX) uses 128 bit registers, and
: is coming out fairly soon (this year or next, I don't remember)  Not the
: same thing as addressing 128 bits, but this is just the first step in the
: move that'll surely take us there eventually.  Soon followed, I'm sure,
: by 128 bit FP registers on some CPU for the power number crunchers (heck,
: maybe even on Merced for all we know)


Here is two uses where 128 bits may be used.

	1). Simulating a nuclear explosion inside a computer model vs.
	     the real thing. You would need to keep track of all the
	     atomic particles that are created and destroyed during the
	     simulation.


	2). Simulating the human body. Since the human genome project
	    is slowly being completed ( I don't know what percentage
	    is left to do) one could take all of this genetic information
	    and put it into a computer model and simulate all the genes
	    and chromosomes interacting with each other to create
	    human life within the computer. The cells would multiply
	   and create a full sized human being within the computer.
	   We could speed up the growth rate so we could see how we
	   age within hours vs decades; how manipulating the genes
	   will change the human computer model  or using virus gene
	   computer models and seeing how the
	   genes interact with ours; be able to find out how the human
	   being learns by teaching this computer model (the AI people happy)
	   etc..... 
	   So the possibilities are unlimited as to what we could do with
	   the human gene computer model. There wouldn't be any
	   ethical problems (since it isn't alive and makes the religious
	   people happy), no test animals required (makes the animal 
	   loving people happy) etc.....

	3). And once we decipher the grand unification theory we could
	    simulate those too. Perhaps even simulate the entire universe
	    in the computer model.


Now the only question is.. is 128 bits enough or do we need more????
 
