In article <Eu9sur.AEC@spcuna.spc.edu>, Terry Kennedy <terry@spcunb.spc.edu> writes:
:Geoff Roberts <geoffrobx@stmarksx.ppx.catholicx.edux.aux> writes:
:> Anyone know if it's possible to connect a HSC and a Vaxen using CI without a
:> Star Coupler?
:> I have a feeling it can be done by cross wiring the cables (have them) TX-RX
:> RX-TX at each end, but would like some confirmation before I try it.  There
:> are no other boxes involved, just the VAX 8530/CIBCA and the HSC50.
:> Failing that, I'd settle for a source in Oz where I can get a KDB50 or 2.
:> Cheaply.
:
:  It's an unsupported configuration, but may work depending on the type of
:controller and microcode version involved. Some of the CI adapters want to
:see what they send on the RX line, some don't care.

  This configuration is inadvisable, and particularly with any systems
  and CI hardware that you care about...

  Not only is a CI without a star coupler an unsupported configuration,
  it is possible that the CI receivers on the CI port controllers may be
  overloaded and damaged by such a configuration -- either immediately,
  or eventually...  One should definitely not assume that the CI controller
  transmitter power will match the expectations of the receivers -- well,
  not without the star coupler present to attenuate the signaling...

  And as mentioned, various CI widgets will send themselves a CI message
  as part of the startup self-test -- this loop will rather obviously
  require a functioning star coupler to succeed.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
