Not completely true. 
First reverse transcriptase allows one to write to DNA.
Second (and a pet bugaboo of mine) all we know is how usage of DNA
proceeds (mesage passing as you described above) to transfer info in the
DNA through various bits of RNA till we get a final protein. 
In the absence of gamma ray microscopes, we cannot be sure that other
things are not going on. In particular, consider the problem of
embryology. It's not at all clear (at least based on my limited knowledge
of the subject) that generating an embryo maps obviously onto this
DNA->RNA->proteins metaphor. In particular such knowledge as we do have
(for example that the hox genes seem to be laid in in spatial fashion that
seems to mimic the spatial layout of the embryo) would seem to imply some
different sort of usage of the DNA.

Maynard

