Russell Sasamori <puzzle@shore.net> writes:

> So, what determines what you call the �bitness� of a processor? 

Two possibilities: the size of user-visible pointers, and the width of an
integer register. One concerns the amount of data a program can address
easily, the other the amount of bit parallelism the architecture offers.

It certainly isn't: the width of internal or external busses; the implemented
width of physical or virtual address fields in page tables etc; the size of
the physical or virtual address spaces as seen by privileged code.

