Russell Sasamori wrote:
> So, what determines what you call the �bitness� of a processor?  If a
> processor has a 16-bit internal bus but an 8 bit external bus, you call it
> an 8-bit processor but if it has a 32-bit internal bus and 64-bit external
> bus, you call it a 32-bit processor?  Is SC/MP a 1-bit processor because
> computations are done bit-serially?  If someone designs a processor that can
> just move around a 256-bit data inside of it, is it a 256-bit processor?  Or
> is it the largest size of data that you can perform arithmetic on?  For
> example, do we call 8080 a 16-bit processor because it can perform 16-bit
> arithmetic on the HL register?  Or is it the instruction size?  Is Alpha a
> 32-bit processor because all instructions are 32-bit long?  (this is just a
> friendly discussion)

We have agreed that bitness refers to the architecture. A n bit
processor is one that has n bit wide address registers, even if the
current implementation doesn't support all these bits (e.g. 68k or most
of the current 64 bit processors use only a subset of the address
register width on the bus). The with of the data bus doesn't matter at
all, it's just an implementation detail.

According to this definition, the 4004 is a 12 bit processor (it has 12
bit addresses (?)), and all 808x are 16 bit processors (the 8086 and
8088 with additional segment registers).

