Russell Sasamori <puzzle@shore.net> asks:
>> the conclusion seems to be that 128-bits are useful for
>> some multimedia stuff (AltiVec should demonstrate that),
>> and some crypto work seems to be a lot faster with them.

>George:

>How would cryptography benefit from wider registers and ALU's?
>I thought the most frequently performed operations were emulating LFSR's so I
>cannot imagine how having a 128-bit ALU can present such a big benefit over a
>64-bit ALU, for example.   (Unless the the ALU was custom-designed to do
>cryptography work).

      Some cryptographic algorithms, such as RSA, 
require exponentiation modulo big numbers (about 1024 bits).
Using a 64-bit ALU with unsigned 64 x 64 -> 128 multiplication
(which the Alpha has via its MULQ and UMULH instructions),
we need 256  64 x 64 multiplications to do a 1024 x 1024
multiplication via the grade-school algorithm.
With a 128-bit ALU, the cost becomes 64  128 x 128 multiplications.
The number of operations has dropped by a factor about 4.
Even if each 128-bit operation takes twice as long
as the corresponding 64-bit operation, we gain considerably.

       For these applications, a pipelined integer multiplier
is important.   The inner loop of a 1024 x 1024
multiplication might fetch a 128-bit word from
one operand and multiply it by all eight 128-bit words
from the other operand, getting eight 256-bit products.
We can afford to wait several cycles for each multiplication
to complete if several 128 x 128 multiplications are executing
concurrently.  If instead we must wait for the multiplier
to complete one operation before starting a second multiplication, 
we undergo this delay 16 times (once per low product 
and once per high product).  I have heard rumors that
the 21264 will have a pipelined integer multiplier,
but have not seen such an announcement from DEC.
-- 
        Peter-Lawrence.Montgomery@cwi.nl    San Rafael, California



