Russell Sasamori (puzzle@shore.net) wrote:
: I agree that 8088 and 68008 were considered to be 8-bit processors.Even the
: Intel Data book describes 8088 as an 8-bit processor.

1) The natural data-width of an architecture may well be different from
that of a processor.
2) Data Books are often writeen for hardware folks by marketing folks. This
introduces both a difference in focus and a margin of error.
3) Both the 8088 and 68008 are prehistoric by computer industry standards.
You must be willing to throw out incorrect usage when sampling terminology.

: So, what determines what you call the �bitness� of a processor?

For a processor? It really depends on context. The person designing the
motherboard probably wants to know a bunch of widths: address bus, data
bus, perhaps for both cache and memory, etc. Implementations have gotten
too complex to characterize most modern processors with a single
bus-width. The person designing the ALU wants to know the size of the
inputs and how long a value is expected to be handled in a single step,
etc.

We who write code are usually more interested in in the size of some
fundamental types offered by the software platform we are using. This is
often influenced by the underlying hardware, but it is not necessarily the
same as any underlying hardware bus-width. As such, we are interested in
the data-widths built into architectures. (Both software and hardware.) For
example, the designator for one very widely used API is "Win32" where the
32 indicates 32-bit pointers and integers. It does not say anything about
the underlying processor. (In fact, Win32 runs on processors where all
registers are at least 64-bits and processors which have claim to 48-bit
addressing but also implement a mode where registers are 16-bits. It runs
on processors that have 128-bit or perhaps even 256-bit busses, etc.)

So for architectures, we tend to state the data-width as the size of
integer and address registers, with a (usually) unstated assumption that
these are the same. There are good reasons for having a single register
size for fixed-point data and addresses in general purpose architectures,
but it is hardly written in stone that this must be so. Problems arise
because instead of elaborating on the terminology when we encounter more
complex architectures, e.g. ones using segmented addresses, people tend to
use the same simple descriptions and drop information.

-Z-



