Alex Colvin wrote:
> 
> >> So, what determines what you call the "bitness" of a
> processor?
> 
> as noted elsewhere, the bitness of an *architecture* is
> usually defined by the architecture's register width. the
> rest is implementation, and varies.
> 
> it also wasn't uncommon to refer to the most annoying
> processor limitation. this often was the bus, which was
> a real bottleneck.
> 
> so if i use a high-speed serial disk channel, am i using
> a one-bit processor?
> 
> 

Not if the serial channel is high enough speed or if the disk 
channel is used infrequently enough (or has large buffers/caches 
on the CPU end of the channel). If, however, you are using CPU
that interfaces to all external memory and devices via a serial
bus that has a bandwidth significantly lower than the internal 
CPU bandwidth (as is the case with most CPU/bus combinations) 
then you would, for most intents and purposes, be using only a 
1-bit CPU, no matter how wide the internal registers or address 
word are.

The reason that people referred to the 68008 and 8088 and 8-bit
processors is that that they performed very much like one, no 
matter the size of the internal busses or registers. The same
holds for the 68000/68010 and the 16/32 debate. It wasn't until
quite rescently that the external bus was significantly cached
on the CPU, which is what allows us to be concerned more with the
internal arrangement of the chip rather than the external bus
configuration.

The whole "bitness" thing is similar to the clock speed thing,
where several manufacturers were selling chips that claimed to
be N-MHz chips but were, in terms of bus speed, on N/2 or N/4
MHz chips. The performance hit you took on some of those chips,
for the slow external bus, was quite significant, and put the 
lie to the marketing claims.

- Jeff Dutky



