> >  lfsr = (lfsr << 1) | feedback_term;
> 
> I visualized the circuit in my head but could not figure out what
> it is doing in terms of polynomial algebra.  OTOH this I can understand:
> 
> if(LFSR & 0x8000000000000000)
>  lfsr=(lfsr<<1)^POLYNOMIAL;
> else
>  lfsr=(lfsr<<1);

That isn't a LFSR, it is instead the traditional way to implement CRC!

> >On a 500MHz cpu, this would enable up to about 30+ MB/s, still far below
> >what a cheap FPGA should do with no (very low) host cpu overhead.
> 
> I agree.  Cryptography is one of the fields where you can get a lot
> of mileage out of building a dedicated hardware.
> 
> >The Motorola altivec in-register
> What is altivec?

Motorola's 128-bit wide (extended) MMX/VIS style vector processing
extension.

> >However, most (all?) other crypto algorithms are block algorithms,
> What do you mean by block?
> Do you mean as in "block error correcting code" where the elements
> of the group are a^1, a^2, ... a^n?

No, as in the algorithm is defined as working on BLOCK bits
simultaneously, i.e. using IDEA you have to pad the input data until it
is a multiple of 128 bits long.

Similarly, DES works on 64-bit blocks, by doing 16 iterations of
data-dependent bit permuations within that block, before going on to the
next 64-bit block.

A LFSR can work one bit at a time, and every bit is dependent upon all
preceeding bits.

Terje

