In article <6lg65c$l9n$1@mark.ucdavis.edu>,
Bill Broadley  <bill@math.ucdavis.edu> wrote:
> Yes it's not the pointers that are handy.  I don't know of any proposed
> machines with > 2^64 or ram.  Last I checked 2048 cpu supers needed on
> the order of 40-50 bits of addressing.

You're thinking physical memory. Think VM.

Think an operating system where each program runs at its own unique address
for the lifetime of the system, where the system is a networking cluster.

Use some of the 128 bits for vendor ID, a-la the top 24 bits in an ethernet
address (in fact you could use ethernet vendor IDs), then another 24 bits for
cluster ID, and you've still got more than 64 bits left over for application
instances. Now the whole world looks like a flat address space.

I don't know any killer applications that require this, but it is a model that
would make use of very large address spaces.

-- 
