In article <1998Jun4.014906.1@eisner>,
Rob Young <young_r@eisner.decus.org> wrote:
>	Why NT domination [of engineering workstations]?  Cost.  In
>	particular, savings:
>
>http://www.workstation.digital.com/NT/tco/tco.htm

For anybody who didn't follow this link, it leads to a study of 26
cases in which organizations migrated from Unix workstations to NT
workstations. It doesn't examine any organizations which tried and
failed, or which decided not even to try, or which belong to that tiny
group of iconoclasts which migrate the other direction. Even if one
doesn't suspect that total cost of ownership numbers are exceptionally
easy to skew to prove any point, one suspects that this is like proving
that Buick owners are more satisfied than Honda owners by interviewing
solely a group of people who have just purchased a new Buick.

Readers of EE Times have for a couple of years now withstood a barrage
of articles with the topic "Windows is here!", "Windows is taking
over", "Soon you will have no choice but Windows!". Recently the
magazine published a rather sheepish article reporting that Windows use
in EDA was still low.

I was surprised--I cynically believe that decisions on what tool the
engineers will use are generally made by pointy-haired managers, based
mainly on press hype--but if my experience is any indication, maybe the
NT user experience is so poor that in this case reality actually
defeats hype. I apologize that this has little to do with comp.arch,
but if one allows lies to go unanswered, pretty soon the truth dies
out.

I work for an ERP company, though I've worked at EDA-using companies in
the past.  My company has been a Windows-centric organization for years
(it runs its entire business on NT); my division was Solaris-centric
before we were acquired, but we were already migrating to NT by
managerial fiat, because our customers wanted it, and because "the only
problem with NT compared with Unix is scalability, and that's being
addressed".

The first problem I have with NT is that you have to be a guru just to
log in.  We use NT RAS boxes for dialup networking, and they are set to
require a proprietary version of PPP which excludes most non-Microsoft
systems, even if they only want to talk to other non-Microsoft systems
via the RAS box. You can turn off this "feature", but my sysadmin told
me, "It took them six weeks to get the Windows RAS box to work stably,
so there's no way I'm going to ask them to mess with a single
setting".

So let's say you use a Windows laptop for dialup access.  Don't click
that inviting little check-box that says "log in via dialup"!  It's a
trap--if the laptop was connected to ethernet the last time you used
it, you MUST log in without any networking and run an obscure program
to release your IP address before you try to dial. (The obscure program
is different on Win95 and NT--Microsoft says "Windows everywhere", but
no two versions are alike.) If you forget, you get stuck and you may
have to go to a magic directory and delete a bunch of files before you
can proceed.  The reason for this is that NT is an administrator's
nightmare: you can't just install a new version of a program on a
server, you have to install it on all 4000 employee desktops and
laptops. Our company's solution is to run automated login-time scripts
that download new software onto your machine, but if your laptop
networking settings are in ethernet-mode when they ought to be in
modem-mode, the scripts proceed just far enough to screw things up, and
you're going to be waiting for a callback from the company help desk
before you can log in again.

Let's suppose you manage to log in without incident. Our company uses
NT password aging, which means that every few weeks you either change
your password or discover that you can't log in any more. But in the
Windows environment, your password is kept in myriad places. There's a
cached copy on your machine, plus a copy on the server, plus a copy for
the email program (Lotus Notes in our case), plus a copy for the
source-control program, and so on. Just because you tell the server to
change your password doesn't mean that the new password propagates
anywhere else, so you could spend a lot of your life changing
passwords, or you can learn to use multiple passwords, and live with
the fact that sometimes these programs try to be smart and use your
login password, and other times they don't (e.g. the Lotus client isn't
smart enough to change its password automatically to match that of the
OS, but in some cases it appears to try to use the OS password). Normally
the server password propagates to the cached copy on your machine, but
even there, you can encounter confusion: if you have both a desktop and
a laptop, and you change the server password with the desktop, and you
use the laptop both with modem and ethernet dialup, it may take more
than one login/logout cycle before the laptop suddenly notices that the
password has changed. Be prepared to try both the old and new until the
new works.

When my laptop does use the cached information, I have to bat down
repeated dialog boxes telling me that various network resources aren't
available, even if I tell the first dialog box not to warn me any
more. But that still beats dialing in--for some reason, when the Windows
RAS boxes are heavily loaded, you get in a "walking dead" state where
you're doing PPP just fine, but the authentication process bogs down
and times out. Fortunately our company has lots of sites, so each of
us develops a list of favorite nonlocal sites to dial when ours doesn't
work.

All of this makes logging something of a sport. Often it takes me 15
minutes by phone--feed that into your TCO calculations.

The next usability problem is that the pathname of a file kept on a
server contains the hardwired server name.  If you run out of disk
space on one server, you can't move files to another one, because you
can't use symbolic links or automounting to hide the movement, and you
can't expect hundreds of users to change their scripts to reflect the
new location. Half the programs which run under Windows don't seem to
understand the "\\servername\directory" notation anyway, so everybody
has already agreed to map a drive letter like "m:" to
"\\servername\directory"; if you move some files off of "servername"
onto "otherserver", you will surely break some script that uses both
the files that moved and the files that didn't move, because it can't
map "m:" to both places. My experience with Unix EDA users is that they
constantly move files from one server to another in search of free disk
space or to improve access speed; I would think that using NT would be
crippling for CAD/CAM too.

Another usability problem is that NT seems determined that users should
compute only on their own machines. My experience with Unix EDA users
is that they constantly use "rlogin", "rsh", and the client/server
architecture of X windows to run big slow applications on whatever
machine happens to be free; often, in fact, they write scripts to
discover automatically all the idle machines in the whole network and
run regression tests on them, something that is easy with the Unix
shell and nigh impossible with the DOS shell.

The Windows GUI doesn't have a client/server architecture (people tell
me to wait till the next release of NT with Citrix technology--we'll
see). And forget about "rlogin" and "rsh"--apparently the "rsh"
capability is rendered null by the NT security model and the fact that
if you do succeed in logging into a server this way, it doesn't set up
a copy of your own user environment, so nothing much is likely to
work.  I've been pleading with my management for over a year now just
to give me a working telnet server for text-oriented work, but it
remains flaky (remember, this is a company that runs its entire
business on Microsoft, so if they don't know how to do it, most NT
shops don't). I would also expect these problems to be crippling for
CAD/CAM.

Still another usability problem is that except for "applications", NT
is a tool-starved environment: for many things that one takes for
granted on Unix, one must either port code or buy third-party
products.  We had to port "sendmail" so that our nightly build scripts
could notify a list of users about their success or failure; we had to
buy a Unix tool emulation package because the DOS shell is such a
pathetic excuse for a scripting language; we're about to buy a "pmake"
equivalent; we are about to replace freeware source-code control with a
commercial product; and so on. Another factor for your TCO
computation.

Finally, for an environment with all the security bells and whistles of
NT, it sure seems crash-prone. Our Lotus servers are up and down constantly
(I don't know whether the problem is Lotus or Microsoft, but it sure isn't
anywhere as reliable as Unix mail). And when connected by modem, NT seems
to give high priority to programs which are talking over the modem and
not getting response from the RAS server--so much that I can often freeze
my system to the point where I have a screen full of unrefreshed windows,
and I can't get the task manager to kill the offending process.

I could go on and on boring you with this, but the summary is that
while Windows is a glorious environment for developing GUIs and using
PowerPoint, NT makes it so difficult to do my daily work, that when I
read material like the URL above, I have a strong sense that we're
admiring the emperor's new clothes.

Incidentally, we used to support NT on Alpha, but management decided to
drop it. Though we now support NT only on Intel-32, we have already
announced that we'll support NT on Merced, even though we don't know
anything about Merced. I think this says volumes about the DEC strategy
of putting so many of Alpha's eggs in the NT basket.
-- 
Steven Correll == PO Box 66625, Scotts Valley, CA 95067 == sjc@netcom.com



