Kevin G. Rhoads wrote:
> 
> Please read what I wrote and respond to that.
> 
> If the code gives CORRECT answers with compiler
> optimizations OFF and it gives WRONG answers
> with compiler optimizations ON -- question, where
> is the bug?
> 
> Answer, in the compiler optimizations -- the code
> did NOT change, the compiler did.

When a program works with optimization turned off
but not when optimization is turned on, it usually
means one of two things:

1) The program is an illegal program that is doing
   something that the language definition says is
   illegal or has undefined or unpredictible results.

2) The program is triggering a compiler bug.

It's been my experience that the majority of
"optimizer bugs" turn out to be illegal programs.
It's quite easy to introduce these sorts of problems
into your code.  Just one small Fortran example:

    IF (A .NE. B) .AND. (FUNC(X, Y) .NE. 4)

where FUNC is a function that, as a side effect,
modifies variable Y, followed later by some code
that depends on FUNC having changed Y's value as
a result of the execution of the IF statement.
The Fortran language does not guarantee the order
of evaluation of expressions of equal precedence;
the compiler is free to evaluate the comparison of
A and B first and then shortcut the expression if
they're equal, so that FUNC never gets called.

When optimization is turned off, a simple-minded
full evaluation of the expression might take place,
so that the code seems to "work", but it "breaks"
if you turn on optimization and the compiler decides
to exercise its right not to call FUNC if it can
evaluate the expression without doing so.

This is a case where the code didn't change, but it
is not an optimizer bug.  The program is depending
on a particular result from an operation that is
defined in the language as being unpredictible.

--PSW



