stev knowles wrote:
> it is interesting, i just read some of the multics web pages, and i didnt
> realize (or forgot) that the GE 645 was a multiprocessor. they really were
> ahead of their time. it is actually amazing how far we have slid back ... .

Once the 8086 PC came out, the rest of the computer industry stagnated because
we had to wait for the 8086 to catch up. PC's capable of really talking at
ethernet speeds are relatively recent, even though ethernet has existed since
early 80s. LANs that work are relatively recent, even though VMS clusters
(much more sophisticated) have exsited since the mid 80s. 

PC's have just recently been able to connect to the internet, even though VMS
and UNIX machines have done so for over a decade.

And now that the 8086 is up there with the big boys, I suspect that we will
finally start to see some forwards movement again.

So essentially, the IT industry was on "PAUSE" since the mid 80s, in order to
allow the PC to catch up. The funny thing for the nintendo generation is that
they think that stuff such as Ethernet and Internet are new when in fact they
are quite old.



