Ketil Z Malde <ketil@ii.uib.no> writes:

>Is there *any* potential, imagined problem, where 128bit adressability
>is necessary?  (Well, except for the internet, of course :-)


64 bits isn't enough to directly address all the storage in the world, so
you need to go higher.  Maybe not to 128 bits, but you might as well because
at some point 96 bits won't be enough.

Why directly address all the storage in the world?  I dunno, why not?
Someone will come up with a good reason to do it.  Some probably have
really good reasons already.

I don't see a compelling reason for a 128 bit CPU today, but as transistors
get cheaper and cheaper the cost of doing it will go down to the point
where you won't need many applications that want > 64 bits before it makes
sense to do it.

The Motorola "Altivec" stuff (souped up MMX) uses 128 bit registers, and
is coming out fairly soon (this year or next, I don't remember)  Not the
same thing as addressing 128 bits, but this is just the first step in the
move that'll surely take us there eventually.  Soon followed, I'm sure,
by 128 bit FP registers on some CPU for the power number crunchers (heck,
maybe even on Merced for all we know)

-- 
