I was approached last night at a coffeehouse by someone claiming to
be a chemical engineer for Intel and claiming knowledge of computer
hardware and systems.  However, several of his comments seemed off
the mark if not downright ignorant.  For example, he confused the
difference between the Intel P series (i.e., P6 microprocessor)
with clock rates of the Intel Pentium II series, saying for
example that the "P5" (by which *he* evidently meant Pentium II 500)
wasn't out yet (which it isn't, but that's beside the point).

When I brought up the issue of Intel's acquisition of DEC
chipmaking plants, I mentioned the Alpha, which (again, confusing
clock speed with architecture) he derided as being inferior to the
upcoming Pentium II 500.  Even more bizarre, the basis for this
unfavorable comparison was his claim that the Alpha requires a
bigger fan!  (Hey, never mind floating-point operations, just
give me a computer with a small fan.)   And the reason he said the
"P500" will need a smaller fan is that it is constructed using 
"vertical integration" and therefore "the electrons don't have to
move as far" as with the "horizontal integration" Alpha.  Does 
anyone have any idea what, if anything, he could have been
thinking of, in terms of system architecture?  And what fraction
of heat production does the microprocessor chip account for in
these systems?  I can't recall seeing a microprocessor heat-sinked
like a power transistor, and it seems obvious that the memory and 
peripheral chips and components (particularly the power supply),
taken together, would produce far more heat.  

Also, can someone tell me what nominal voltage levels the Pentium and
the Alpha use for the "high" logic state?  He said it was 1.5 volts 
for the Pentium and 7 volts for the Alpha.  I know nothing about the
voltage requirements for these systems but the rest of his comments
left me wondering whether this could be taken at face value.

For any Intel employees in the greater Phoenix Area who might be able
to confirm or deny his employment with Intel, he claimed to be from
Jordan (though he didn't look Jordanian -- more Eastern European,
though of mixed background I think and with some real personal
knowledge of and experience in the Middle East).  Late 20s, 
said he studied at a technical institute in New York and claimed
to work at an Intel facility in Chandler but to live in Awatukee.


