JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
>> > What on earth would you use 128 bits for?
>
>Adressability might not be the problem. But the ability to transport larger
>blocks of data faster might. (eg: 128bit bus, 128bit registers).
>
>Wouldn't this not make memory operations faster (16 bytes per transfer) ?
>Wouldn't this make disk drive transfer (DMA or otherwise) faster ?

You don't necessarily need 128-bit registers to use 128-bit
or larger buses; as you have caches in between, there's an
easy disconnect point at the memory interface.  Sun's UltraSPARC
chips all use 128-bit buses and 64-bit registers (unless the
Ultra IIi doesn't, but I don't have the spec convenient).

I've been having this argument offline with some people;
the conclusion seems to be that 128-bits are useful for
some multimedia stuff (AltiVec should demonstrate that),
and some crypto work seems to be a lot faster with them.
I was less than entirely enthusiastic about the idea,
but since then AltiVec was announced and presumably
Motorola did the detailed tradeoff studies to justify it,
so it appears that my feelings were wrong.


-george william herbert
gherbert@crl.com




