I agree absolutely. But consider the following case:

      PROGRAM TEST

      CALL SUB(1)
      CALL SUB(2)

      END      

      SUBROUTINE SUB(I)

      INTEGER I,J,K

      IF (I.EQ.1) J=2

      K = J + J

      RETURN

      END

(please excuse old-style F77).

It's perfectly possible that many compilers at different levels of
optimisation will have K=4 from the second call to SUB. Yes, 2+2 always
= 4. But K = -123e+78 from the above example would not be an optimiser
bug.

I've also run into this when compiling code as a DLL rather than an EXE,
with the same (or no) optimisation in both cases. (I'd point out that
this was not with a DEC compiler.)

