> : >Is there *any* potential, imagined problem, where 128bit adressability
> : >is necessary?  (Well, except for the internet, of course :-)
>
> I believe OS/400 is designed to scale easily to a 128 bit address space,
> allowing the relatively painless move from 48 bits to 64 bits.  

The AS/400, according to Soltis' book,
has 16 byte (128 bit) pointers,
of which 96 bits are reserved for addresses,
and of which only 64 bits are used as address at the moment,
on the PowerPC based implementations.

Pre-PowerPC "only" 48 bits of address were used,
and apparently they were running out.

Note: an AS/400 segment is 16 mega-bytes (24 bits),
and appears to be the smallest unit of allocation of address space.
The AS/400 does not do garbage collection - an address, once
allocated, remains allocated to the end of time (or, formerly,
to the next IPL (boot)), although physical memory is reclaimed.

Of the 32 non-address bits in the 128 bit point, apparently
they were formerly used as authorities (capabilities),
although this has been phased out in user code (although it
still exists in system code).




