ok i couldnt pass this one up

>Note that one DMA molecule is only around 30-50 times as large as an
>atom, so 2^128 bits are just a somewhat larger mountain.

first of all, there is no fixed size for a 'DNA molecule'. I can certainly
make a very simple one with only two base pairs which is quite small,
but take a chromosome from a human or something omcplex, and you have
hundreds of millions or billions of pairs. Each pair is composed of two
bonded proteins, each of which has many atoms. How can anythign but the 
simplest DNA molecule possible be only 30-50 times as big as an atom?

second, as big as what atom? an atom of uranium of 200 times bigger than a
hydrogen atom, far bigger a variance than what you give as the factor of
scale between a DNA molecule and 'an atom'. 

There are DNA chains which are large enough to be be detected with optical
instruments. 

There are instances where an unsigned 32 bit integer would barely hold the
count of the number of bits of in a single strand of DNA.
actually _storing_ this data is a totally different matter altogether! 
youd have to store a couple billion bits _per_ strand. (4 bits per base pair)

anyways, 2^128 is certainly big. 

happy hacking
