Keith - first, thanks so very much for MOLDY.

Re data corruption -- I believe the key is later in your
post where you say:
"I should mention that this is compiled with "-g -O2 -automatic" -- the
problem goes away at lower optimization levels."
This really sounds like an optimizer bug.  

"More mysteriously exactly the same behaviour is observed
when the code is compiled with f90 instead of f77" not
mysterious if they are from the same vendor, in which case 
they likely share the same back-end, where all the low level 
optimizations are performed.

I have found that on non-trivial code (anything with more than 
a few thousand lines), whether it be C or Fortran, that you 
are fairly likely to hit some optimizer bug.  Numerical computations
tend to be the most likely to trigger optimizer bugs (just my personal
experience, not from any studies).

I have made it a rule for some years now to compile EVERYTHING
with optimizations off as well as on and compare the results of
standard test runs.  This last year I have been bumping into a new
optimizer bug roughly once every other month.  (Of course, I have
been running the code through three different vendor's Fortran 
compilers and six different vendor's C compilers.)

Now most of my coding these days is on x86/Wintel platforms, 
and if you are using workstations I expect the compilers are 
much more solid.  But, even so, there will be optimizer bugs.

If it is an optimizer bug, the only long-term fix is getting
a corrected compiler from the vendor.  In the short-term,
since you've found that putting in debugging writes clears
the problem up, may I humbly suggest that opening a NULL
device for output and leaving those WRITEs in, but directed to 
NULL, may be a useable work-around (with, of course, suitably
scathing comments about the sloppiness of the compiler vendor's
coders and Q/A testing . . .  ;-)  )  Alternatively turn off some 
of the optimizations for the module in question. 
-- 
Kevin G. Rhoads, Ph.D.
