"Kevin G. Rhoads" <T_Rhoads@NoSpam.CLASSIC.MSN.COM> writes:


> If the code gives CORRECT answers with compiler optimizations OFF
> and it gives WRONG answers with compiler optimizations ON --
> question, where is the bug?

I know this seems like an obvious conclusion but I am afraid in my
(many years of) experience it is false.  One obvious way this might
happen is if you omitted to initialize a variable.  Compiling with no
optimization will often result in it being initialized to zero -- even
though you didn't ask for it in your code.  However the optimized code
will pick up whatever value was last in some register or memory
location and will therefore give different results.  Another mistake
is to pass the same actual argument twice to two different dummy
arguments in a subroutine.  The standard entitles the compiler to
assume that these refer to distinct areas of store and optimizing on
that assumption may not produce the results you intended.  Yet another
faulty but common assumption is that variables retain their values
between invocations of a subprogram.


> Answer, in the compiler optimizations -- the code did NOT change,
> the compiler did.


But incorrect code with "undefined" behaviour may yet produce
"correct" results.  Perhaps the language lawyers don't make this point
sufficiently clear.....

Nevertheless I believe the part of code that I am having trouble with
and started this thread about does *not* suffer from failure to
initialize, aliasing or assumptions of SAVE behaviour or array bounds
errors (at least not in the part where the faulty result occurs). Does
anyone have any further suggestions as to what to look for?  If not
then maybe this really is one of the relatively rare cases of a
genuine optimizer bug.

Keith refson



