Ah, if the world was exactly that simple.

There are many things in language specs that are "undefined".
This means the compiler is free to to as it will on code
that invokes undefined behaviour.

A good optimiser will push the letter of the law with
the language, if by doing so it can improve performance
(however you measure "performance").  In doing so, it
could well change the results you get from the non-compliant
code.  Both results are consistent with the word "undefined
behaviour".
 
