Simple example: most people with prior exposure to C are prone to
writing things like

    if ((x /= 0) .and. (test_condition_assuming_x_not_0 (x))) then
       ....
    end if

since most compilers don't reorder boolean expressions, this bug will
not be noticed until you hit a compiler (like DEC's) that takes
advantage of Fortran's fredom to reorder booleans.  I have had this
(in numerous disguises) more than once.

> Are you just flaming?  Or do you have some justification you are not
> sharing with the rest of us?

Careful.  Forgetting to SAVE variables is a _very_ common mistake.  It
still happens occasionally to me and the response to the -automatic
switch is a smoking gun when a student shows up with this problem
[believe me: almost all do at one point in their career ...]

True anecdote:

    A few months back I tested a new fortran compiler on some old code
    that worked on IBMs with VM and MVS, on VAXen with VMS, and on more
    flavors of UNIX that I can remember.  Everywhere the results where
    (up to floating point differences) identical.
    
    The new compiler appeared to produce complete garbage and I was
    ready to blame the compiler.  Then I tried the -static switch (the
    opposite of -automatic on this compiler) and the code produced
    correct results.  Then it took very little time to track down a
    forgotten SAVE. 
