I totally agree about the dangers of uninitialised variables, array
bound errors and the like, and particularly about the incorrectness of
assuming that if it works with compiler A, but not with compiler B, then
compiler B is bugged - but...

... this one looks like a compiler bug to me.  I'd be happier if I knew
the value if POSTMP(1) and POSION before the assignment to NGRPX, but
subject to that, and taking into account the checks the author has made,
it's difficult to explain any other way.  I suppose NGRPX/Y/Z might be
aliased, or the optimiser might have reordered code so that something
with an aliasing implication was executed after the 1101 loop and before
you inspected POSTMP, but that's a long shot.

I have found bugs just like this, even down to the WRITE making it go
away.  The act of printing forces the optimiser to regularise things, so
values get put back where they belong.  One trick I used successfully
(on a completely different platform) was to pass the offending variable
to a subroutine which prints it, rather than printing directly.  However
you've already got the evidence using the debugger.  What does the
compiler vendor say?
