That is just as silly as asserting the error MUST be
the compiler.

I did not mean to assert either such silliness.

Although, in response to the ignorant, unhelpful
and totally lacking in substance or information
post made several posts back, I simplified
the reasoning so the flamer could (hopefully)
understand it.  In so doing I simplified it to
the point of error -- oversimplified to death.

You now, are guilty of the same:
>	If your program gives correct results with optimization *and*
>	-noautomatic, then the bug is almost always in the code.
It takes MORE information than just that.

If you had summarized that by asserting that when
-noautomatic or a 
global SAVE  
make purported optimizer bugs disappear that is
a strong sign of code bugs not optimizer bugs
then I would agree.  

But as stated, it is oversimplified and subject to
misinterpretation.  Just as was my response to
Jeffrey's flame.

So -- let me make clear --
AFTER the code has been carefully checked
(and more than once)
and preferably after the code has had a review
by other than it's author (not always possible)
and AFTER testing for 
uninitialized variables,  
non-persistent variables treated as persistent, 
parameter list mismatches or 
the same variable being passed by reference to two or more 
different formal parameters

and, if possible,
after testing the code on multiple platforms with
multiple compilers

then there comes a point when one has a reasonable
expectation that maybe there is a compiler bug and
that eventuality should be tested.

This is NOT the first thing to suspect.
Nor the second or third, but it does go on the
list of possibles!

Jeffery's post assert it NEVER appears on the list
of things to suspect and check, and in replying to
that silliness  I oversimplified.  

Suspicion of a compiler bug DOES belong in the
list of things to check -- but it should be WAY DOWN
the list.  
