billm@cygnus.com (Bill Moyer) writes:

>  There are a few new things under the sun:
>  * Alpha now runs a popular operating system (WNT),

It has since NT 3.51 came out, didn't it?  Certainly since 4.0 came out,
which is several years now.  That's not new.  Oddly enough, Linux on
Alpha may be more important for its initial market penetration than NT,
at least if MS continues to treat it like a temporary necessary evil to
be tolerated until Merced comes out.

>  * Alpha can run many popular applications,

Not many new ones in the past couple years though, I'll bet.  MS isn't
even bothering to port all their NT software to it; which gives little
incentive for vendors not already doing so to continue doing so.  Yeah,
I know about FX!32, but it doesn't work for drivers, and despite all
the guarantees a business won't trust critical software to it when they
can just buy a dual processor Pentium II for the same performance (or
dual Xeon when the 21264 ships for real)

>  * Alpha has come down in price to the point that home users can afford it,

Granted, this has changed in the past couple years.  Though I doubt very
many "home users" will be interested.  Other than computer professionals,
not many people use NT at home.  It isn't nearly as easy to buy some
random hardware and have it work as with an x86 PC running Win95, because
hardware vendors test with Win95 and x86, but not often with NT and hardly
ever with Alpha.  Now if a killer new game came out that blew everything
else away but _required_ an Alpha, you might have a shot.  Or at least
that's the impression I get, considering the guesses I've seen on annual
spending on upgrades by ardent gamers.  I think business PCs are the best
shot for Alpha penetration into the NT market.

