That's what I meant -- "non home".  I didn't say Engineering CAD/CAM in
particular because you also have graphics (the only place left where Apple
really has a big market share) and specialty stuff like quantitative
analysis for Wall Streeters.  Certainly no one will buy one as a standard
corporate PC for office & productivity stuff.  AMD & Cyrix have brought
PC prices down faster than MS can bloat their software, at least for the
moment :)

The problem for Engineering CAD/CAM in particular is that this is a market
where Unix was completely dominant until a few years ago.  NT came along
and the guys who weren't performance sensitive went to PCs.  If they aren't
performance sensitive, why get an Alpha and introduce support headaches by
having another version of NT to support?  Unless they think they can dump
Unix entirely, they could just as well continue with whatever Unix platform
they've settled on -- they won't switch to Digital Unix because people
aren't that confident it'll be around in a few years.  Its been said there
is only room for 2 or 3 commercial versions of Unix a couple years down the
road, which leaves Solaris, HP-UX and maybe AIX -- I see little chance for
Digital Unix or Irix to have more than 2% each by 2001.  It is unfortunate
that the Alpha's fate is in the hands of Microsoft, but that's how I see
it.  Perhaps the Merced delay announcement may get MS a bit more interested
in NT.  I wonder how much Compaq would have to bribe them to do 64 bit NT
on Alpha ASAP, instead of waiting for Merced to come out?


>	Arana will ship well before Merced does.  So will 21364.
>	And I believe 21464 is due about the time of Merced's lastet
>	slip into the future.  A plethora of Alpha parts to fit into
>	workstation, server segments not unlike the X86 line today.  And
>	when the low-end part (.18 21264) outperforms Merced, there will
>	be more than a modest chance of Alpha success as we approach
>	the Merced (mid-2000) timeframe.


What is Arana?  The 21364 _may_ ship before Merced, but you are in
dreamland if you think Digital can get two major new architectures out
in two years.  The 21264 is way behind schedule, it is barely meeting
the term "shipping" right now.  Digital had a good record on meeting
their expected ship dates from the '064 and '164.  They really have
blown it with the '264.  I see no reason to think that the '364 will be
here any sooner than the two years they had between the '064 and '164.
The clock starts when they start shipping the '264 in quantity, so
summer 2000 is the earliest I'd believe the '364 to be shipping in
quantity.  Maybe they'll surprise us, but with all the distractions
they've had in the past year or two, I wouldn't bet on it.

