In article <6ko4p2$1p2a$1@piglet.cc.uic.edu> amaliy1@uic.edu (Anil T Maliyekke) writes:
>Considering that AlphaNT based systems weren't able to show a 
>performance advantage over PPro servers for most commercial apps, 
>it doesn't seem likely that the 21264 will improve things much,
>at least not until a 64 bit NT gets here.  

In those cases where the 21164 doesn't shine against the PPro servers,
it is mostly cases where the instruction footprint of the commercial
application does fit into the Pentium Pro L2 cache (which has pretty
high bandwidth and holds more instructions per given capacity due
to IA32's higher code density) but not the 96 Kbyte of L2 of the
21164.

The 21264 has a *much* large and faster L2 cache, hence it isn't
hampered at all by this occasional achilles heel. So expect the
21264 to make a *big* difference even for those few commercial
applications where the 21164 could not smoke a Pentium Pro.

>Even with that,
>things like bandwidth bottle necks (system, disk and network)
>will get in the way.  

You may want to have a look at the system bandwidth of 21264 systems
when they eventually (sigh) become available.

