> What surprises me is that there are no SPEC95 numbers of those systems yet.
> Does that mean that they are more than half a year away (the maximum "advance
> warning" allowed by SPEC) ?  Or is Digital waiting for SPEC98 because the
> 21264 will shine off better with the new benchmarks ?

  Somewhere in the Samsung web pages is a list of "speed grades" of the 21264
  with the fastest part attributed with 43 SPECint95 and 64 SPECfp95.  I put
  "speed grades" in quotes because the different parts were identified by
  relative performance to a 500 MHz 21164 rather than by clock rate.  Also,
  Samsung didn't identify the chipset (presumably tsunami), the size of L2,
  or the system and cache bus speeds.

  DEC and its partner(s) have been pretty careful about not being pinned down
  on specifics on the 21264.  Given the previous straight forward manner in
  which DEC normally discloses such information it raises questions about what
  exact state is the 21264 in.  Maybe DEC is having a horrible time getting
  manufacturable yields at the target frequencies or perhaps latent logic bugs
  have prevented operation with all planned instruction issue combinations
  enabled.  It might even be as prosaic as delays in getting fully functional
  chipset.  Maybe non-technical reasons are involved such as program management
  difficulties (official merced delay cause) or perhaps an intentional desire to
  provide a fuzzy target for HP and Sun to aim the PA-8500 and US-III at; or they
  want to make a big splash by shipping with higher than expected benchmarks
  (like PPro intro).

  I hope DEC comes clean pretty soon with real benchmarks even if only from an
  evaluation board.  This evasiveness from a normally open and straight forward
  company is rather troubling.

