I have just wasted about 3 days attempting to debug a really
mysterious problem with a FORTRAN program on DU 4.0D.  The original
problem is that the program gives different results when compiled with
the "-automatic" flag to put variables on the stack.

However irrespective of any bugs in the program I have narrowed the
focus to one particular segment of code which is behaving in an
inexplicable manner.  Namely: a computed result is wrong - as deduced
from extra debugging "write" statements"  Yet when I add a "write"
statement to print the disputed value, both it and the final result
are now correct.    The code looks like:

      DO 1100 NI=1,NIONSP(NSP)
      DO 1101 IM=1,3
      POSTMP(IM)=MOD(10.D0+POSION(IM,NI,NSP),1.D0)  
 1101 CONTINUE
c      write(6,9999) postmp
c 9999  format(3f10.5)
      NGRPX=1+NINT(NGX*POSTMP(1))
      NGRPY=1+NINT(NGY*POSTMP(2))
      NGRPZ=1+NINT(NGZ*POSTMP(3))

Using the debugger on the immediately following statement yeilds:

(ladebug) p postmp
(1) 10.033126
(2) 0.0127600000000001
(3) 0.03312599999999932

(ladebug) p posion(1,1,1)
0.009953999999999999
(ladebug) p posion(2,1,1)
0.01276
(ladebug) p posion(3,1,1)
0.033126
(ladebug) next

I should mention that this is compiled with "-g -O2 -automatic" -- the
problem goes away at lower optimization levels. I'm fairly sure
that this erroneous value in postmp(1) IS being stored and used later
on - even if the array postmp is being optimized away since later
results are consistent with use of the erroneous value.

I had to step on a fair way before the optimized code thought it had
finished with that block of code, after which:
 
(ladebug) p ngrpx,ngrpy,ngrpz
503 1 2

The values of NGX,Y,Z=50,32,27 so this is consistent however postmp
has changed suggesting that it is register allocated or otherwise
optimzed away.


(ladebug) p postmp
(1) 0.03312599999999932
(2) 4.940656458412465e-323
(3) 0.03312599658966064

As I mentioned before, uncommenting the "write" statement both prints
out the correct value and fixed the problem.

I can't see any way this could be a bug in the code. Obviously I have
checked for array bounds problems, uninitialized variables and found
nothing.  More mysteriously exactly the same behaviour is observed
when the code is compiled with f90 instead of f77.   This is a verly
large code needing about 300MB of variable space (stack or data) to
run, and of course I have configured the kernel with sufficient
stack/data limits etc for the data.

A Deja news search revealed a 1996 article by Toon Moene 

Subject:      Stack allocation hosed in Digital Unix 3.2C ?
From:         toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl (Toon Moene)
Date:         1996/02/02
Message-ID:   <1057@moene.indiv.nluug.nl>
and Message-ID:   <1058@moene.indiv.nluug.nl>

where he described a similar-sounding problem caused by a machine
configuration error as shipped by Digital, namely the value of the
"stackinc" kernel symbol.  kdbx reveals it still (in 4.0D) has the
value he thought was wrong, namely 32767.  However upping it has
made no difference.

Has anyone encountered a similar problem and/or know of a fix?

sincerely

